\h Exodus
\toc1 Exodus
\toc2 Exodus
\toc3 Exo.
\mt1 Exodus
\is1 Introduction
\ip The word \bk Exodus\bk* comes from the Greek word meaning ‘departure’ or ‘leaving’. Hebrew readers call this document ‘Shemōt’ which is the second word in the Hebrew and means ‘Names’ because it begins with a list of names. Some European and other translations call it ‘The second book of Mosheh/Moses’.
\ip It should be noted that ‘Mosheh’ is the best English transliteration of the name of the hero of this account. Sadly, he's commonly known in the English world as ‘Moses’, even though we have all the sounds in our language to be able to pronounce his name more accurately. Unfortunately, because the language of ‘the New Testament’ didn't have the ‘sh’ sound and had no letter for ‘h’, his Hebrew version of his Egyptian name is corrupted as it goes through Greek ‘Mōsaʸs’ and then Latin ‘Moyses’ and then first entered English in the 1300's as ‘Moises’. Now that we live in a modern era where we try to show more respect to the names of peoples from other cultures, the \em OET\em* encourages the same for Biblical characters (especially the major ones), hence ‘Mosheh’. (Maybe a little bit of relearning could demonstrate a lot of respect to the Jewish people whose ancestors are recorded in these accounts.)
\ip After God had used a famine to cause his people to go down into Egypt (Heb. Mitsrayim), this document records how they eventually become slaves, but then God works many miracles to enable them to leave again, and not only that, to be paid at the end for their work.
\ip This story can be divided into three main parts: 1. their slavery in Egypt, and how God called Mosheh (Moses), the child who had been adopted by an Egyptian princess, to lead them out of slavery; 2. how God assisted them when they were chased by the Egyptian army, then when they reached Mt. Sinai, how they were miraculously given food and drink there at the mountain; 3. how Yahweh revealed himself to Mosheh there at Mt. Sinai, and gave him instructions to guide their lives and their nation, and God's agreement with the people; 4. the ending is the construction of the sacred tent, complete with instructions about how they should worship.
\ip An overview would be how God rescued his people from slavery and established them as a nation that was hopeful of a good future.
\ip \em Mosheh\em* is the most important person in this account. He was chosen by God to lead his people out of slavery and right out of Egypt. The \em ten commands\em* are another well-known component of this document and can be found in chapter twenty.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Yisrael's descendants are driven out of Egypt \ior 1:1–15:21\ior*
\io2 a. The people are enslaved there in Egypt \ior 1:1-22\ior*
\io2 b. The birth and early life of Mosheh \ior 2:1–4:31\ior*
\io2 c. Far'oh (Pharaoh) king of Egypt stands strongly against Mosheh and Aharon taking the people out of Egypt \ior 5:1–11:10\ior*
\io2 d. God's messenger of death that ‘passed-over’, and the exit of Yisra'el's descendants from Egypt \ior 12:1–15:21\ior*
\io1 Leaving the Red Sea and going to Mt. Sinai \ior 15:22–18:27\ior*
\io1 God's instructions and the agreement \ior 19:1–24:18\ior*
\io1 The sacred tent and warnings concerning worshipping \ior 25:1–40:38\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Yisra'el's descendants are driven into slavery
\rem /s1 The Israelites Are Treated Cruelly in Egypt; The Israelites Oppressed; The Israelites in Egypt
\p
\v 1 These¦25401 are¦25402 the¦25402 names¦25402 of¦25402 Yisra'el's¦25404 descendants¦25403 who¦25405 accompanied Yacob (Jacob) when¦25410 he moved his¦25410 household¦25410 to Egypt¦25406:\x + \xo 1:1-4: \xt Gen 46:8-27.\x*
\v 2 Reuben¦25413, Simeon¦25414, Levi¦25415, Yehudah¦25416,
\v 3 Yissakar (Issachar), Zebulun¦25419, Benyamin¦25420,
\v 4 Dan¦25423, Naftali¦25424, Gad¦25425, and¦25426 Asher¦25426.
\v 5 \add At that time,\add* Yacob¦25435 had a total of seventy¦25436 descendants (including¦25438 Yosef¦25438 \add and his sons\add* who¦25432 were¦25439 \add already\add* in¦25440 Egypt¦25440.)
\p
\v 6 \add As the years progressed,\add* Yosef¦25443 and¦25444 his¦25446 brothers¦25446 died¦25442—all of that¦25449 generation¦25448,
\v 7 \add but they'd had many children who went on to have their own children and grandchildren,\add* and so they became¦25456 a large and¦25459 powerful¦25456 group \add there in Egypt\add*.\x + \xo 1:7: \xt Acts 7:17.\x*
\rem /s1 The Israelites Are Oppressed
\p
\v 8 \add Eventually\add* a¦25465 new¦25467 king¦25465 \add ≈came to power\add* in Egypt¦25470 \add !(Mitsrayim¦25470)\add* who¦25471 had never known¦25474 Yosef¦25477\x + \xo 1:8: \xt Acts 7:18.\x*
\v 9 and¦25479 he¦25479 told¦25479 his¦25482 people, “Listen, those descendants of Yisrael¦25486 \add !(Israel¦25486)\add* are¦25487 becoming more¦25489 numerous¦25487 and¦25488 powerful¦25488 than¦25489 us¦25489.
\v 10 \add So\add*, let's¦25492 deal¦25492 wisely¦25492 with them, in case they \add continue to\add* multiply¦25496. Then¦25497 if¦25498 war¦25501 was¦25502 to¦25506 break out, they could easily take the other side and¦25509 fight¦25509 against¦25511 us¦25511, and¦25512 then leave¦25512 \add &our¦25515\add* country¦25515.”\x + \xo 1:10: \xt Acts 7:19.\x*
\v 11 Then¦25517 they¦25517 appointed¦25517 overseers¦25519 to work them in slave¦25520 gangs¦25520, and using them as¦25521 forced¦25523 labour¦25523, they built¦25524 the store¦25526 cities¦25525 at Pithon and¦25531 Rameses¦25533 for Far'oh (Pharaoh).
\v 12 \add However,\add* the more they oppressed¦25536 them, the more they multiplied¦25539 and¦25540 spread¦25541 out, and¦25542 \add the more\add* the \add @Egyptians\add* came to dread¦25542 the¦25544 Israelis¦25545.
\v 13 \add ≈So¦25547\add* \add they\add* made the¦25551 Israelis¦25552 work¦25547 \add long, hard hours\add*,
\v 14 and¦25555 they¦25555 made¦25555 their¦25558 lives¦25558 bitter¦25555 with¦25559 the hard¦25560 slavery. \add They were forced to make\add* mortar¦25561 and¦25562 bricks¦25562, as well as all¦25563 kinds of slave labour¦25565 in¦25566 the¦25566 \add #fields¦25566\add*—all¦25568 the work¦25570 they¦25573 had to do was \add strenuous and tiring\add*.
\p
\v 15 Then¦25577 the¦25578 king¦25578 of¦25578 Egypt¦25579 spoke¦25577 to Shifrah¦25585 and¦25586 Puah¦25588, the two Hebrew¦25581 midwives¦25580,
\v 16 saying¦25590, “When¦25591 you¦25591 assist¦25591 the¦25594 Hebrew¦25594 women¦25594 as midwives¦25591, \add as you sit there on your\add* stool, if you¦25595 see¦25595 that it's a¦25601 male, \add ≈do something so he'll die\add*, but if it's a¦25607 female, then¦25609 let her live¦25609.”
\v 17 But¦25611 the¦25612 midwives¦25612 \add were godly women\add* and¦25616 didn't¦25616 do¦25617 according to¦25620 what the¦25621 Egyptian¦25622 king¦25621 \add had instructed\add* them, and¦25623 they¦25623 let¦25623 the boys live¦25623.
\v 18 \add ≈So¦25628\add* the¦25629 king¦25629 called¦25628 the midwives¦25632 \add back\add*, and¦25633 he¦25633 \add demanded\add*, “Why¦25635 have¦25636 you¦25636 done¦25636 that¦25638 and¦25639 let¦25639 \add their\add* sons live¦25639?”
\p
\v 19 “Because¦25649 the¦25653 Hebrew¦25653 women aren't¦25650 like¦25651 Egyptian¦25652 women¦25653,” the¦25661 midwives¦25661 \add ≈answered¦25644\add*. “They're¦25657 \add more active\add* and¦25662 give¦25662 birth¦25662 before¦25658 the midwife \add even\add* gets¦25659 to¦25660 them¦25660.”
\v 20 \add ≈So¦25664\add* God¦25665 rewarded the midwives¦25666, and¦25667 \add meanwhile,\add* the¦25668 people¦25668 \add continued to increase in number\add* and¦25669 become¦25669 more powerful¦25669,
\v 21 and¦25672 because¦25673 the¦25676 midwives¦25676 had \add demonstrated their desire to obey\add* God, he¦25680 gave them¦25681 families¦25682 \add of their own\add*.
\v 22 Then¦25684 Far'oh¦25685 commanded¦25684 all¦25686 of¦25688 his¦25688 people¦25688, “You¦25695 \add all need to\add* toss¦25695 every¦25690 newborn boy¦25692 into¦25694 the¦25694 river¦25694, but¦25696 you¦25699 can let¦25699 the girls¦25698 live¦25699.”\x + \xo 1:22: \xt Acts 7:19.\x*
\c 2
