\rem /s1 Moses; The Birth of Moses; Birth and Youth of Moses
\p
\v 1 \add During this period,\add* a¦25703 man¦25703 from the¦25704 tribe of¦25705 Levi¦25705 married a woman who was also¦25706 a Levite¦25711,
\v 2 and¦25713 she¦25713 conceived¦25713 and¦25715 gave¦25715 birth¦25715 to a¦25716 son¦25716. When¦25717 she¦25717 saw¦25717 that¦25719 he¦25722 was¦25721 a \add healthy\add* boy, she¦25723 hid¦25723 him¦25723 away for three¦25724 months¦25725,\x + \xo 2:2: \xt Acts 7:20; Heb 11:23.\x*
\v 3 \add but\add* then¦25727 she¦25729 wasn't able¦25729 to¦25731 hide¦25731 him¦25731 any longer. \add So\add* she¦25732 got¦25732 a¦25735 basket¦25735 made from \add woven\add* reeds and¦25737 plastered¦25737 it¦25737 with¦25738 bitumen¦25738 and¦25739 pitch¦25739. Then¦25740 she¦25740 put¦25740 the¦25744 baby¦25744 in¦25741 it¦25741, and¦25745 floated \add the basket\add* in the¦25749 reeds along the¦25750 riverbank,
\v 4 \add leaving the baby's\add* sister¦25753 to watch from¦25754 a¦25754 distance¦25754 to¦25755 see¦25755 what¦25756 would¦25758 happen to¦25759 him¦25759.
\p
\v 5 \add After a while,\add* Far'oh's¦25764 daughter¦25762 came¦25761 down¦25761 to¦25765 the¦25768 river¦25768 to wash \add herself\add*, and¦25769 \add she and\add* her¦25769 young \add attendants\add* were¦25770 walking¦25770 along the \add riverbank\add*. She¦25775 saw¦25775 the¦25778 container among the reeds¦25780 and¦25781 sent¦25781 \add one of\add* her¦25784 slave¦25784 women to get it¦25785.
\v 6 She¦25787 opened¦25787 it and¦25788 saw¦25788 the¦25791 baby¦25791 inside, and¦25792 wow¦25792, he \add started\add* crying¦25795. She¦25796 felt \add ≈sorry¦25796\add* for him¦25797 and¦25798 said¦25798, “This¦25801 baby \add must be\add* one¦25799 of¦25799 the¦25799 Hebrew¦25800 children¦25799.”
\p
\v 7 Then¦25803 his¦25804 sister¦25804 \add approached and\add* asked¦25803 \add the princess\add*, “Would you like me to¦25805 go¦25810 and¦25811 find a¦25813 Hebrew¦25816 woman¦25816 who'll be able to¦25818 breastfeed¦25814 the¦25821 baby¦25821 for you?”
\p
\v 8 “\add Yes,\add* go¦25829,” answered¦25823 Far'oh's¦25828 daughter¦25826, and¦25830 the¦25831 girl¦25831 went¦25830 and¦25832 got the¦25835 baby's mother¦25835.
\v 9 “Take¦25843 this¦25847 baby¦25846,” said¦25838 the¦25840 \add princess\add*, “and¦25848 breastfeed him¦25848 for¦25839 me¦25849, and¦25850 I'll¦25851 pay¦25851 you \add for doing it\add*.” \add So\add* the¦25856 woman¦25856 took¦25855 the¦25857 baby¦25857 and¦25858 \add ≈looked after\add* him¦25858.
\v 10 \add When\add* the¦25862 boy had grown¦25860 \add enough\add*, she¦25863 brought¦25863 him¦25863 \add back\add* to¦25864 Far'oh's¦25866 daughter¦25864 and¦25867 he¦25867 became her¦25869 son¦25870.\x + \xo 2:10: \xt Acts 7:21.\x* She¦25871 named¦25871 him ‘Mosheh¦25873’\f + \fr 2:10 \ft More familiar to most English readers as ‘Moses’ from the Greek ‘Μωσῆς’ (Mōsaʸs) but Greek doesn't have an ‘h’ or a ‘sh’ so by going through Greek we ended up with something quite different from his real name. However, English does have those sounds and letters, so there's no reason why we can't get this name correct.\f* \add (which means ‘pulled out’)\add* because¦25875 she¦25874 said¦25874 that she'd \add plucked\add* him out of the \add river\add*.
\s1 Mosheh escapes to Midyan
\rem /s1 Moses Escapes to Midian; Moses Flees to Midian
\p
\v 11 \add Later on\add* when Mosheh¦25886 was fully grown¦25885, he¦25887 went¦25887 out¦25887 to¦25888 visit \add the Hebrews\add* and¦25891 saw¦25891 their¦25892 \add forced labour\add*, and¦25893 he¦25893 saw¦25893 an Egyptian¦25895 man beating¦25896 a¦25897 Hebrew¦25899 man—one¦25900 of¦25900 his¦25900 own people.\x + \xo 2:11-14: \xt Acts 7:23-28.\x*
\v 12 \add @Mosheh\add* looked¦25905 around to check that¦25906 no one was¦25907 watching, then¦25909 he¦25909 hit¦25909 the¦25912 Egyptian¦25912, \add killing him,\add* then¦25913 he¦25913 hid¦25913 his body in¦25914 the sand¦25914.
\v 13 The¦25917 next day¦25917, he¦25916 went¦25916 out¦25916 \add again\add* and¦25919 wow¦25919, two¦25920 Hebrew¦25923 men¦25922 were¦25924 fighting¦25924 each other, and¦25925 he¦25925 said¦25925 to¦25926 the man in the wrong, “Why are¦25928 you¦25928 hitting¦25928 your¦25929 \add fellow Hebrew\add*?”
\p
\v 14 “Who¦25932 made you the ruler and¦25936 judge¦25936 over¦25937 us¦25937?” \add the man replied\add*. “Are you \add planning\add* to¦25938 kill¦25938 me¦25938 like¦25941 you¦25942 killed¦25942 \add that\add* Egyptian¦25945?” Then¦25946 Mosheh¦25947 was¦25946 afraid¦25946 because he¦25948 realised¦25940 that what he'd done¦25933 had \add probably become widely\add* known¦25950.
\v 15 \add Indeed,\add* when¦25953 Far'oh¦25954 heard¦25953 about it, he \add ordered\add* Mosheh to¦25960 be killed¦25960. \add So\add* Mosheh¦25963 had to flee¦25964 from¦25966 \add the king\add* and¦25968 he¦25968 \add took off east\add* to live¦25968 in¦25969 Midian¦25971\x + \xo 2:15: \xt Acts 7:29; Heb 11:27.\x* and¦25972 he¦25972 stayed¦25972 near the¦25975 well¦25975.
\p
\v 16 The¦25977 priest¦25977 there in Midian¦25978 had seven¦25979 daughters¦25980, and¦25981 they¦25981 would come¦25981 to the well to draw¦25982 water¦25987 out and¦25983 fill¦25983 up the¦25986 troughs¦25986 there so their¦25989 father's¦25989 \add sheep and goats\add* could drink¦25986.
\v 17 Now some male shepherds¦25992 came¦25991 along and¦25993 \add started to\add* shoo their¦26000 flock¦26000 away¦25993, but¦25994 Mosheh¦25995 got involved and¦25996 helped them¦25996 so¦25997 that their animals could drink.
\v 18 \add When\add* they¦26002 \add got home\add* to¦26003 their¦26006 father¦26006 Reuel¦26005, he¦26007 \add asked\add*, “How come¦26010 you \add got home\add* so early today?”
\p
\v 19 “There was an Egyptian¦26015 man¦26014,” they¦26013 \add ≈answered¦26013\add*, “who stood up for us¦26016 against those \add other\add* shepherds¦26018. And¦26019 he¦26022 even drew¦26022 \add water\add* for¦26023 us¦26023 and¦26024 gave \add &our¦26027\add* flock¦26027 water¦26024 to drink.”
\p
\v 20 “Where¦26033 is¦26033 he \add now\add*?” he \add asked them\add*. “What's¦26034 this¦26035—you mean you all just left¦26036 him there? Go and get him \add so we can give him a meal\add*.”
\p
\v 21 \add Later it turned out that\add* Mosheh¦26046 was¦26045 prepared to¦26047 live¦26047 with¦26048 the¦26050 man¦26050, and¦26051 \add in due course\add* he¦26051 gave¦26051 his¦26055 daughter¦26055 Zipporah¦26054 to¦26056 Mosheh¦26056 \add in marriage\add*.
\v 22 \add When\add* she¦26058 gave¦26058 birth¦26058 to a¦26059 son¦26059, he¦26060 named¦26063 him ‘Gershom¦26064’ \add (which means ‘foreigner’)\add* because¦26065 he¦26066 said¦26066, “I've¦26068 become¦26068 a foreigner \add living\add* in¦26069 a foreign¦26070 land¦26069.”
\p
\v 23 \add Eventually\add* Egypt's¦26079 king¦26078 died¦26077, \add but\add* the¦26081 Israelis¦26083 groaned¦26080 from¦26084 the¦26086 slavery \add they were still under\add* and¦26087 they¦26087 cried¦26087 out¦26087, and their¦26089 cry¦26089 \add for freedom\add* from slavery went¦26088 up¦26088 to¦26090 God.
\v 24 \add *He\add* heard¦26097 their¦26101 groaning¦26101 and¦26102 remembered¦26102 his¦26106 agreement¦26106 with¦26107 Abraham¦26109, with¦26110 Yitshak¦26112, and¦26113 with¦26113 Yacob¦26115,\x + \xo 2:24: \xt Gen 15:13-14.\x*
\v 25 and¦26117 \add *he\add* \add looked down on\add* the¦26121 Israelis¦26122 and¦26123 \add *he\add* \add was concerned about them\add*.
\c 3
\s1 God calls Mosheh from a burning bush
\rem /s1 God Calls Moses; Moses at the Burning Bush; Moses and the Burning Bush
\p
\v 1 \add One time\add* Mosheh (Moses) was¦26128 shepherding the¦26132 flock¦26132 of¦26132 his¦26134 father-in-law¦26134 Yetro (or Jethro¦26133, the¦26135 priest¦26135 at Midian¦26136), and¦26137 he¦26137 led the¦26140 flock¦26140 beyond the¦26142 wilderness¦26142 and¦26143 came¦26143 to¦26144 a hill. (\add This was later known as\add* the¦26146 mountain¦26146 of¦26146 God at Horev¦26148).
\v 2 \add While he was there.\add* Yahweh's¦26152 messenger¦26151 appeared¦26150 to¦26153 him¦26153 in¦26154 a¦26154 flame¦26154 coming from¦26157 the¦26157 middle¦26157 of¦26157 a bush¦26158, and¦26159 as \add @Mosheh¦26159\add* looked¦26159, to his surprise \add he saw that\add* the¦26161 bush¦26161 was¦26162 burning¦26162 in the fire yet not¦26165 \add actually\add* being¦26166 burnt up.\x + \xo 3:2-10: \xt Acts 7:30-34.\x*
\v 3 “I've got to go and see¦26173 this¦26178 amazing sight¦26176,” Mosheh¦26169 said¦26168 \add to himself\add*. “How come that¦26173 bush¦26183 isn't¦26180 burning¦26182 up?”
\p
\v 4 When¦26185 Yahweh¦26186 saw¦26185 that¦26187 he'd¦26188 left his path in order to¦26189 look¦26189, God¦26192 called¦26190 to¦26191 him¦26191 from¦26193 the¦26193 middle¦26193 of¦26193 the¦26194 bush¦26194, “Mosheh¦26196, Mosheh¦26197.”
\p “I'm¦26199 here¦26199,” he¦26198 \add ≈replied¦26198\add*.
\p
\v 5 “Don't¦26202 come¦26204 any closer,” \add @Yahweh¦26201\add* said¦26201. “Take¦26206 off¦26206 your¦26208 sandals¦26208 because¦26211 the¦26212 place¦26212 where you're¦26214 standing¦26215 is¦26217 HOLY ground¦26217.
\v 6 I'm¦26223 the¦26224 god¦26224 of¦26224 your¦26225 father¦26225 and the¦26226 god¦26226 of¦26226 Abraham¦26227, Yitshak¦26229, and¦26230 Yacob¦26231.” \add So\add* Mosheh¦26233 covered hid¦26232 his¦26234 face¦26234 because¦26235 he¦26236 was¦26236 scared¦26236 to¦26238 look¦26237 at God.
\p
\v 7 “I've¦26245 certainly noticed¦26245 the¦26248 suffering¦26248 of¦26248 my¦26249 people¦26249 in¦26251 Egypt¦26251,” Yahweh¦26243 \add continued\add*, “I've heard¦26255 their¦26254 \add #cries¦26254\add* as the \add slave drivers\add* oppress them—I'm¦26255 unmistakingly aware¦26259 of their \add #pain¦26262\add*.
\v 8 \add ≈So¦26264\add* I've¦26264 come¦26264 down¦26264 to¦26265 set them free from¦26267 Egyptian¦26268 \add ≈control\add* and¦26269 to¦26269 bring¦26269 them up¦26269 from¦26270 \add there\add* to¦26274 a¦26276 good¦26277 and¦26278 wide¦26278 land—a¦26281 land¦26281 flowing¦26282 with milk¦26283 and¦26284 honey¦26284. \add It's currently\add* the¦26287 place¦26287 of¦26287 the¦26288 Canaanites¦26288, the¦26289 Hittites, the¦26290 Amorites, the¦26291 Perizzites, the¦26292 Hivites, and¦26293 the¦26293 Yebusites¦26293.
\v 9 \add Yes,\add* the¦26297 cry¦26297 of¦26297 the¦26298 Israelis¦26300 has¦26301 now¦26295 \add reached\add* me¦26302 and¦26303 what's more, I've¦26305 seen¦26305 \add how much\add* the¦26308 Egyptians are¦26311 oppressing¦26311 them¦26312.
\v 10 \add ≈So¦26314\add* go¦26315 now¦26314 and¦26316 I'll¦26316 send¦26316 you¦26316 to¦26317 Far'oh (Pharaoh) and¦26320 bring¦26320 my¦26323 people¦26324—Yisra'el's¦26326 descendants—out¦26320 from¦26327 Egypt¦26327.”
\p
\v 11 “Who¦26334 am¦26335 I¦26335, that¦26336 I¦26337 should go¦26337 to¦26338 Far'oh¦26340,” Mosheh¦26330 \add asked\add* God, “and¦26341 that I¦26342 should \add be the one to\add* bring¦26342 out¦26342 the¦26345 Israelis¦26346 out from¦26347 Egypt¦26347?”
\p
\v 12 “I'll¦26352 certainly be¦26352 with¦26353 you,” \add @God\add* \add replied,\add* “and¦26354 this¦26354 will¦26357 be¦26357 your sign¦26357 \add so that you'll know\add* that¦26358 I have sent¦26360 you¦26360: when¦26361 you¦26361 bring¦26361 the¦26364 people¦26364 out¦26361 from¦26365 Egypt¦26365, you¦26366 all will¦26366 serve¦26366 God on¦26370 this¦26372 \add very\add* hill¦26371.”
\rem /s1 The Divine Name Revealed
\p
\v 13 “\add But listen\add*,” Mosheh¦26375 told¦26374 God, “\add If\add* I'm¦26380 about¦26381 to¦26381 go¦26381 to¦26382 the¦26384 Israelis¦26385 and¦26386 if I¦26386 told¦26386 them¦26387, ‘Your¦26389 ancestors¦26389' god has¦26390 sent¦26390 me¦26390 to¦26391 you¦26391.’ then¦26392 they'd¦26392 \add ask\add* me¦26394, ‘\add Oh yeah,\add* what's¦26395 his¦26397 name¦26397?’ then what¦26398 would¦26399 I¦26399 tell¦26399 them¦26400?”\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Exo 6:2-3.\x*
\p
\v 14 “I¦26407 AM¦26407 WHO¦26408 I¦26409 AM¦26409,” God¦26403 told¦26410 Mosheh¦26406. “You'll¦26412 \add tell them this\add*: ‘I¦26415 AM¦26415 (Yahweh) sent¦26416 me¦26416 to¦26417 you¦26417.’ ”\x + \xo 3:14: \xt Rev 1:4,8.\x*
\v 15 Then¦26419 \add *he¦26424\add* \add continued\add*, “This is what you¦26427 \add need\add* to¦26428 tell the¦26430 Israelis¦26431: ‘Yahweh¦26432, the¦26433 god¦26433 of¦26433 your¦26434 ancestors¦26434, the¦26435 god¦26435 of¦26435 Abraham¦26436, Yitshak¦26438, and¦26439 Yacob¦26440, sent¦26441 me¦26441 to¦26442 you¦26442. This¦26443 is¦26445 my¦26445 name¦26445 forever, and¦26447 this¦26447 is¦26448 my¦26448 memorial¦26448 throughout the generations.’
\v 16 Go¦26452 and¦26453 gather¦26453 the¦26456 Israeli¦26457 elders¦26456, and¦26458 tell¦26458 them¦26459, ‘Yahweh¦26460, the¦26461 god¦26461 of¦26461 your¦26462 ancestors¦26462, appeared¦26463 to¦26464 me¦26464, the¦26465 god¦26465 of¦26465 Abraham¦26466, Yitshak¦26467, and¦26468 Yacob¦26468, to¦26469 say¦26469, “I've¦26471 certainly noticed you¦26472 all and¦26473 \add how you're all being treated\add* in¦26477 Egypt¦26477,
\v 17 and¦26479 I've¦26479 promised to take¦26480 you¦26481 all from¦26482 your mistreatment in Egypt¦26483, away to¦26484 the¦26486 land¦26486 where the¦26487 Canaanites¦26487, Hittites, Amorites, Perizzites, Hivites, and the Yebusites¦26492 \add are currently living\add*—to¦26493 a¦26495 land¦26495 flowing¦26496 with milk¦26497 and¦26498 honey¦26498.” ’
\p
\v 18 Then¦26500 \add the people\add* will¦26500 listen¦26500 to¦26501 what you tell them, and¦26502 you¦26503 and¦26504 the¦26504 Israeli¦26505 elders¦26504 will¦26502 go¦26502 to¦26506 the¦26508 Egyptian¦26509 king¦26508 and¦26510 you'll¦26510 all tell¦26510 him¦26511, ‘Yahweh¦26512, the¦26513 god¦26513 of¦26513 the¦26514 Hebrews¦26514, has¦26515 met¦26515 with¦26516 us¦26516. \add So\add* now¦26517, please¦26520 let¦26518 us¦26518 go¦26518 three¦26522 days¦26523' journey¦26521 into¦26524 the wilderness¦26524, so¦26525 we¦26525 can sacrifice¦26525 to¦26526 Yahweh¦26526, our¦26527 god.’
\v 19 \add But\add* I know¦26530 that¦26531 the¦26536 Egyptian¦26537 king¦26536 won't let¦26534 you¦26535 go¦26538 \add until he's forced to\add*.
\v 20 \add ≈So¦26543\add* \add I will use force\add* and¦26547 I'll¦26547 strike¦26547 Egypt¦26550 with \add several supernatural acts\add*, and¦26556 after¦26556 those, he'll¦26559 let¦26559 you all¦26551 go¦26559.
\p
\v 21 Then¦26562 I'll¦26562 \add make\add* the Egyptian¦26570 \add people favour you all in such a way that\add* when¦26571 you¦26573 go¦26573, you¦26575 \add won't leave empty-handed\add*.\x + \xo 3:21-22: \xt Exo 12:35-36.\x*
\v 22 \add For example,\add* an \add Israeli\add* woman¦26579 will¦26578 ask¦26578 her¦26580 \add Egyptian\add* neighbour¦26580 (or from¦26581 someone staying \add where she lives\add*) for gold¦26587 and silver¦26585 items¦26586 and¦26588 for clothing¦26588. Then¦26589 you'll¦26589 put¦26589 them¦26589 on¦26590 your¦26592 sons¦26592 and¦26593 daughters¦26595, and¦26596 \add in this way\add* you'll¦26596 all plunder¦26596 the Egyptians.”
\c 4
\s1 God gives Mosheh three miracles
\rem /s1 Signs for Moses; Moses' Miraculous Power; Signs of the Lord's Power; God Gives Moses Miraculous Power
\p
\v 1 “But¦26601 listen,” Mosheh¦26602 responded, “they¦26607 won't believe¦26607 me¦26608 and¦26609 won't¦26609 listen¦26610 to¦26611 \add what I say\add*, because¦26612 they'll¦26613 say¦26613, ‘Yahweh¦26618 never showed himself to¦26617 you¦26617.’ ”
\v 2 “\add Well,\add* what's that in¦26626 your¦26626 hand¦26626?” Yahweh¦26622 \add asked\add*.
\p “\add &My\add* \add shepherd's\add* staff¦26628,” he¦26627 said¦26627.
\v 3 “Throw¦26631 it¦26631 on the ground¦26632,” \add @Yahweh\add* said¦26630. \add So Mosheh\add* he¦26633 threw¦26633 it¦26633 onto the ground¦26634 and¦26635 it¦26635 became a¦26636 snake¦26636, and¦26637 \add he quickly moved back away from it\add*.
\v 4 “Pick it up by its tail.” Yahweh¦26642 told¦26641 \add *him¦26645\add*. \add So\add* \add @Mosheh\add* picked¦26652 it¦26653 up by the tail and¦26654 it¦26654 became a¦26655 staff¦26655 in¦26656 his¦26656 hand \add again\add*.
\v 5 “That's so¦26658 that¦26658 they'll¦26659 believe¦26659 that¦26660 \add I\add* appeared¦26662 to¦26663 you¦26663: Yahweh¦26664—the¦26665 god¦26665 of¦26665 their¦26666 ancestors¦26666—the¦26667 god¦26667 of¦26667 Abraham¦26668, Yitshak¦26670, and¦26671 Yacob¦26672.”
\p
\v 6 “\add Now\add* put¦26678 your hand¦26681 \add on your chest—\add*inside your¦26681 robe.” Yahweh¦26675 added. \add So\add* \add @Mosheh¦26682\add* \add did that\add*, and¦26683 when¦26686 he¦26686 pulled it¦26686 out¦26686 again¦26677, his¦26688 hand¦26688 had gone \add totally\add* white with disease.
\v 7 Then¦26692 \add @Yahweh¦26692\add* said¦26692, “\add Now, put\add* your hand¦26699 \add back in again\add*.” \add So\add* \add @Mosheh¦26702\add* \add did it again,\add* and¦26703 when he¦26703 pulled it¦26703 out¦26703, wow¦26705, it¦26707 had¦26707 \add returned to normal\add*.
\v 8 “\add ≈So¦26710,\add* if¦26711 they¦26714 don't believe¦26714 you¦26715, and¦26716 \add if the first miracle doesn't convince them\add*, then¦26721 they'll¦26721 believe¦26721 \add that second miracle\add*.
\v 9 And¦26726 then, even if¦26727 they¦26730 don't believe¦26730 those¦26734 two¦26732 miracles¦26733 and¦26735 \add won't believe what you tell them\add*, then¦26738 you¦26738 should take¦26738 some¦26739 water¦26739 from the¦26740 river¦26740 and¦26741 pour¦26741 it onto the¦26742 dry¦26742 land¦26742. Then¦26743 the¦26744 water¦26744 which¦26745 came from¦26747 the¦26749 river¦26749 will¦26750 \add turn into\add* blood¦26751 \add there\add* on¦26752 the dry land¦26752.”
\p
\v 10 “Oh my¦26760 master¦26760,” Mosheh¦26755 \add ≈responded¦26754\add*, “I'm not¦26761 \add very good at speaking to others—I never have been and still aren't. I seem to get tongue-tied easily\add*.”
\p
\v 11 “Who \add was it that made people's mouths\add*?” Yahweh¦26785 said¦26784. “Who makes \add someone\add* mute¦26795 or¦26796 deaf¦26797, or¦26798 seeing¦26799 or¦26800 blind¦26801? Isn't¦26802 it me, Yahweh¦26804?
\v 12 \add ≈So¦26806\add* get going¦26807 now¦26806, and¦26808 I'll¦26809 \add ≈look after\add* your¦26812 mouth¦26812 and¦26813 \add show\add* you¦26813 what you¦26815 to say¦26815.”
\p
\v 13 \add But\add* \add @Mosheh\add* \add replied\add*, “Oh master¦26819, please¦26822 send¦26825 \add someone else\add*.”
\p
\v 14 Then¦26827 Yahweh¦26830 \add ≈got angry¦26829\add* with Mosheh¦26831 and¦26832 told¦26832 him, “Isn't¦26833 Aharon (Aaron) a fellow Levite¦26836 and your¦26835 brother¦26835? I¦26837 know¦26837 that¦26838 he¦26841 can speak¦26841 well¦26840, and¦26843 \add what's\add* more, he'll¦26846 come¦26847 \add from Egypt\add* to¦26848 meet¦26848 you, and \add when he does, he'll be very happy\add*.
\v 15 \add He'll become your speaker\add* and¦26853 you'll¦26853 be able to talk¦26853 to¦26854 him¦26854 and \add tell him what to say\add*. I'll¦26861 be¦26861 \add helping both of you speak\add* and I'll¦26868 teach¦26868 you¦26869 both what you¦26872 should do¦26872.
\v 16 \add ≈So¦26874\add* \add in that way,\add* he'll¦26874 be¦26881 like a¦26886 mouth¦26886 for you and speak¦26874 to¦26877 the¦26880 people¦26880 for¦26885 you¦26885, and¦26887 you'll¦26888 be like God¦26891 to him.
\v 17 \add In addition,\add* you'll¦26900 have that¦26899 staff¦26895 with you¦26897 in¦26898 order to do¦26900 those miracles¦26905.”
\s1 Mosheh returns to Egypt
\rem /s1 Moses Returns to Egypt
\p
\v 18 Then¦26908 Mosheh¦26909 went¦26908 back to¦26912 his¦26915 father-in-law¦26915 Yetro (Jethro¦26914), and¦26916 told¦26916 him¦26917, “Can I please¦26919 leave so¦26920 I¦26920 can return¦26920 to¦26921 my¦26923 \add relatives\add* in¦26926 Egypt¦26926 and¦26927 see¦26927 whether they're still¦26928 alive¦26929.”
\p “Go¦26933 in peace¦26934,” Yetro \add ≈answered\add*.
\p
\v 19 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh¦26937 had told¦26936 Mosheh¦26940 in¦26941 Midian¦26941, “Leave¦26942 here and return¦26943 to Egypt¦26944 because¦26945 all¦26948 those who¦26951 \add wanted to kill you\add* are \add now\add* dead.”
\v 20 Then¦26956 Mosheh¦26957 took¦26956 his¦26960 wife¦26960 and¦26961 sons¦26963 and¦26964 put them¦26964 on¦26965 a donkey¦26967 and¦26968 returned¦26968 towards¦26969 Egypt¦26970 \add with them\add*, and¦26971 Mosheh¦26972 took¦26971 the¦26975 staff¦26975 \add that God had turned into a snake\add*.
\p
\v 21 \add On the way,\add* Yahweh¦26980 said¦26979 to¦26981 Mosheh¦26983, “When¦26984 you¦26984 return¦26985 to¦26986 Egypt¦26986, all¦26988 those miracles¦26990 that¦26991 I¦26993 gave you¦26995, you'll be able to \add demonstrate\add* them¦26995 in¦26996 front¦26996 of¦26996 Far'oh (Pharaoh), but¦26998 as for me, I'm going to \add ≈make¦26999 him stubborn\add* \add so that\add* he¦27004 won't¦27003 let¦27004 \add &your¦27007\add* people¦27007 leave.
\v 22 \add But\add* tell¦27009 Far'oh¦27012, ‘Yahweh¦27015 has stated that Yisra'el¦27018 is¦27016 \add like\add* his oldest¦27017 son¦27016
\v 23 and¦27020 \add %he¦27020\add* asked¦27020 you to let¦27022 that son go so¦27026 that¦27026 he¦27026 can serve¦27026 \add %him¦27026\add*. But¦27027 you¦27027 refused¦27027 to¦27028 let¦27028 \add @Yisra'el¦27028\add* go¦27028, so \add @Yahweh¦27030\add* will kill¦27031 \em your¦27034\em* oldest¦27035 son.’ ”\x + \xo 4:23: \xt Exo 12:29.\x*
\p
\v 24 \add One night\add* as they were¦27037 \add overnighting at\add* a lodging place¦27039 near the road, Yahweh¦27041 \add confronted Mosheh\add* and¦27042 would have killed¦27043 him¦27043,
\v 25 \add but his wife\add* Zipporah¦27046 took¦27045 a¦27047 flint¦27047 \add knife\add* and¦27048 cut¦27048 off¦27048 her¦27052 son's¦27052 foreskin¦27051 and¦27053 \add threw\add* it at \add @Mosheh's¦27053\add* feet¦27054 saying¦27055, “You're definitely a¦27057 bloody¦27059 \add husband\add* to¦27061 me¦27061.”
\v 26 (That was referring to¦27069 circumcision¦27069.) \add So\add* then¦27065 \add @Yahweh¦27066\add* \add left them alone\add*.
\p
\v 27 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh¦27073 had told¦27072 Aharon¦27076, “Go¦27077 \add out\add* into¦27080 the¦27080 wilderness¦27080 to¦27078 meet¦27078 Mosheh¦27079.” \add So\add* he¦27081 went¦27081 and¦27082 met¦27082 him¦27082 at¦27083 the¦27083 hill¦27083 of¦27083 God, and¦27085 he¦27085 kissed¦27085 him¦27087.
\v 28 Then¦27089 Mosheh¦27090 told¦27089 Aharon¦27091 \add everything\add* that Yahweh¦27096 had told him and how he¦27098 had¦27098 sent¦27098 him¦27098, and¦27099 all¦27100 the¦27102 miracles¦27102 that¦27103 he¦27104 commanded¦27104 him¦27104 \add to demonstrate\add*.
\p
\v 29 Then¦27106 Mosheh¦27107 and¦27108 Aharon¦27108 went¦27106 \add to Egypt\add* and¦27109 they¦27109 assembled¦27109 all¦27112 the¦27114 \add Israeli\add* elders¦27114
\v 30 and¦27118 Aharon¦27119 told¦27118 them \add everything\add* that¦27124 Yahweh¦27127 had¦27126 spoken¦27126 to¦27128 Mosheh¦27130. Then¦27131 \add @Mosheh¦27131\add* did¦27131 the¦27132 miracles¦27132 in front of¦27133 \add them\add*,
\v 31 and¦27136 the¦27137 people¦27137 believed¦27136. Then¦27138 they¦27138 heard¦27138 how Yahweh¦27142 had¦27141 \add ≈paid attention\add* to the¦27145 Israelis¦27146 and¦27147 that¦27139 he¦27148 had¦27148 seen¦27148 their¦27151 suffering, and¦27152 they¦27152 bowed¦27152 and¦27153 prostrated themselves \add in thankfulness\add*.
\c 5
\s1 Mosheh challenges the Egyptian king
\rem /s1 Bricks Without Straw; Moses and Aaron Speak to Pharaoh; Moses and Aaron before the King of Egypt; Bricks without Straw
\p
\v 1 After¦27156 that¦27174, Mosheh¦27158 and¦27159 Aharon¦27159 went¦27157 in and¦27160 said¦27160 to¦27161 Far'oh¦27163, “This is what Yisrael's¦27169 god¦27168 Yahweh¦27167 says¦27166: ‘Let¦27170 my¦27173 people¦27173 go¦27170, \add so\add* they¦27174 can celebrate¦27174 me¦27175 \add out\add* in¦27176 the wilderness¦27176.’ ”
\p
\v 2 \add But the king\add* said¦27178, “Who's¦27180 \add this\add* ‘Yahweh¦27181’ that¦27182 I¦27183 should listen¦27183 to¦27184 \add him telling me\add* to let¦27185 Yisrael¦27188 go? I¦27190 don't know¦27190 Yahweh¦27193, and¦27194 what's more, I¦27199 won't let¦27199 \add the Israelis\add* go¦27199 \add anywhere\add*.”
\p
\v 3 “The¦27202 god¦27202 of¦27202 \add us\add* Hebrews¦27203 has¦27204 met¦27204 with¦27205 us¦27205,” they \add ≈replied¦27201\add*, “Please¦27207, let¦27206 us¦27206 go¦27206 three¦27209 days¦27210 into¦27211 the wilderness¦27211 so¦27212 we¦27212 can sacrifice¦27212 to¦27213 our god Yahweh¦27213, \add because we don't want him to strike\add* us¦27217 with¦27218 \add ≈deadly diseases\add* or¦27219 with¦27220 \add war\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “Mosheh¦27227 and¦27228 Aharon¦27228,” the¦27224 king¦27224 of¦27224 Egypt¦27225 answered¦27222, “why are¦27229 you¦27229 causing¦27229 the¦27232 people¦27232 to \add slacken\add* off from¦27233 their tasks? \add Get back\add* to¦27235 your¦27235 \add work\add*.”
\v 5 And¦27237 Far'oh¦27238 \add continued\add*, “Listen, there's¦27239 many¦27241 \add of you\add* and¦27245 you¦27245 \add two\add* have¦27245 caused¦27245 them¦27246 to¦27245 rest from¦27247 their¦27247 \add duties\add*.”
\rem /s1 Making Bricks without Straw
\p
\v 6 Then¦27249 on that¦27252 \add very same\add* day¦27251, Far'oh¦27250 ordered¦27249 the¦27255 people's¦27256 slave masters and¦27257 supervisors,
\v 7 “Don't¦27262 give¦27264 straw¦27265 to¦27266 the people¦27266 to¦27267 make¦27267 bricks with as¦27269 \add you've done up till now\add*. They¦27271 can go¦27272 and¦27273 gather¦27273 straw for themselves.
\v 8 But¦27277 \add keep\add* the¦27279 \add same\add* quota of¦27279 bricks¦27280 \add as before\add*—don't¦27288 decrease¦27289 it¦27290 because¦27291 they \add must be\add* idle¦27293 \add if they have time to say\add*, ‘Let¦27301 us¦27301 go¦27301—let¦27302 us¦27302 sacrifice¦27302 to¦27303 our¦27303 god¦27303.’
\v 9 \add Give the men more work so they'll be too busy to worry about deceptive words\add*.”
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦27320\add* the¦27321 slave masters and supervisors of¦27321 the¦27322 people¦27322 went¦27320 out¦27320 and¦27324 told¦27324 \add them all\add*, “\add The king\add* has said¦27328 that he¦27330 won't¦27332 give¦27333 you¦27334 any more straw¦27335,
\v 11 \add so\add* you'll¦27337 \add all have to\add* go¦27338 and get your own straw¦27341 from¦27342 wherever¦27342 you¦27343 can find¦27343 it, because¦27344 there'll¦27345 be¦27345 no reduction to your¦27347 \add quota\add*.”
\v 12 \add ≈So¦27350\add* the¦27351 people¦27351 dispersed throughout all¦27352 of¦27354 Egypt¦27355 to¦27356 gather¦27356 stubble¦27357 for¦27358 straw¦27358,
\v 13 and¦27360 the¦27360 taskmasters¦27360 were¦27361 \add hassling\add* them¦27361, saying¦27362, “\add Hurry up and\add* finish¦27363 your¦27364 day's work¦27364, the same as¦27369 when¦27370 \add you were given\add* straw¦27371.”
\v 14 Then¦27373 the¦27374 Israeli¦27376 foremen¦27374 that Far'oh's¦27382 slave masters had¦27379 appointed¦27379 were¦27373 beaten¦27373 and told¦27383, “Why¦27384 haven't you¦27386 met your¦27387 required brick¦27388 making¦27388 \add quota\add* yesterday or today? \add It's just the same as in the past\add*.”
\p
\v 15 \add ≈So¦27398\add* the¦27399 Israeli¦27401 foremen¦27399 went¦27398 and¦27402 complained to¦27403 Far'oh¦27405, “Why are¦27408 you¦27408 treating your¦27410 servants¦27410 like this?
\v 16 We haven't been¦27414 given¦27414 any straw¦27412 and¦27416 yet they're telling¦27417 us¦27418 to keep making¦27419 bricks¦27416. And¦27420 see¦27420, we your¦27421 servants¦27421 get beaten¦27422, but¦27423 it's your people's mistake.”
\p
\v 17 “You're all lazy—plain lazy!” \add the king responded\add*. “That's why you're¦27433 saying¦27434, ‘Let¦27435 us¦27435 go¦27435 sacrifice¦27436 to¦27437 Yahweh¦27437.’
\v 18 \add ≈So¦27439\add* go¦27440 now¦27439! \add Back to\add* work¦27441! And¦27442 you won't¦27443 be¦27445 given¦27445 straw¦27442, but you¦27446 \add still have to meet the quota\add* of¦27447 bricks¦27448.”
\p
\v 19 \add When\add* the¦27452 Israeli¦27455 foremen¦27452 heard that the¦27453 quota wasn't going to¦27458 be reduced, they \add realised how serious\add* \add &their\add* \add problem was\add*,
\v 20 \add ≈so¦27468\add* when¦27477 they¦27477 left¦27477 Far'oh¦27479 they \add confronted\add* Mosheh¦27471 and¦27472 Aharon¦27474 who were waiting to¦27476 meet¦27476 them¦27476,
\v 21 “May¦27483 Yahweh¦27484 \add see what you've done and judge you for it\add* because you've¦27488 \add raised a big stink in Far'oh's mind\add* and¦27494 with his¦27495 servants¦27495. \add Now they have their excuse\add* to¦27500 kill¦27500 us¦27500!”
\s1 Mosheh complains to God
\rem /s1 Moses Complains to the Lord; God Promises Deliverance
\p
\v 22 Then¦27502 Mosheh¦27503 went back to¦27504 Yahweh¦27506 and¦27507 \add asked\add*, “My¦27508 master¦27508, why¦27509 have¦27510 you¦27510 \add taken it out on\add* \add &our\add* people¦27511? Was this why you¦27515 sent¦27515 me¦27515?
\v 23 \add Ever\add* since I¦27518 went¦27518 to¦27519 Far'oh¦27521 to¦27522 \add deliver your message\add*, he's¦27524 \add made\add* these people¦27525 \add suffer\add* and¦27527 you¦27530 \add clearly\add* haven't rescued¦27530 \add them\add*.”
\c 6
\rem /s1 Israel's Deliverance Assured; Promises of Deliverance
\p
\v 1 Then¦27535 Yahweh¦27536 said¦27535 to¦27537 Mosheh¦27539, “Now¦27540 you'll¦27541 see¦27541 what I'll¦27543 do¦27543 to¦27544 Far'oh¦27544. \add ≈I'll use my power to force him to\add* let¦27548 them¦27548 go¦27548, and¦27549 then \add ≈using my power again\add* he'll¦27551 \add actually\add* drive¦27551 them¦27551 out¦27551 of¦27552 his¦27552 \add country\add*.”
\s1 God promises to rescue the people
\rem /s1 God Calls Moses
\p
\v 2 Then¦27555 God¦27556 spoke¦27555 to¦27557 Mosheh¦27559 \add again\add*, “I¦27562 am¦27563 Yahweh¦27563
\v 3 and¦27565 I¦27565 \add revealed myself\add* to¦27566 Abraham¦27568, to¦27569 Isaac¦27571, and¦27572 to¦27572 Yacob¦27574 as El Shaddai (God¦27575 the provider) but¦27577 they didn't¦27579 know¦27580 me by my¦27577 name¦27577 Yahweh¦27578.\x + \xo 6:2-3: \xt Gen 17:1; 28:3; 35:11; Exo 3:13-15.\x*
\v 4 \add What's more,\add* I¦27584 made¦27584 an agreement¦27587 with¦27588 them¦27588: to¦27589 give¦27589 them¦27590 the¦27593 Canaan¦27594 region—the¦27596 area which¦27598 they¦27600 had been staying¦27600 in¦27601.
\v 5 \add Furthermore,\add* I've¦27606 heard¦27606 the¦27609 complaints from the¦27610 Israelis¦27611 who¦27612 have been¦27614 enslaved by the Egyptians, and¦27616 I¦27616 have¦27616 remembered¦27616 my¦27619 agreement¦27619.
\v 6 Therefore, tell¦27622 the¦27623 Israelis¦27625, ‘I¦27627 am¦27628 Yahweh¦27628 and I will \add help you escape from\add* the¦27632 \add control\add* of¦27632 the Egyptians and¦27634 rescue¦27634 you¦27635 from¦27636 their¦27636 slavery. I'll¦27637 buy you¦27638 out¦27629 of slavery using my \add ≈power\add* and by¦27639 \add punishing the Egyptians for this injustice\add*.
\v 7 I'll¦27644 take¦27644 you¦27645 all to be my own people¦27647, and¦27648 I'll¦27648 be¦27648 your god¦27650. Then¦27651 you'll¦27651 know¦27651 that¦27652 I¦27653 am¦27654 Yahweh¦27654 your god—the one rescuing you¦27657 all from¦27658 the¦27659 Egyptian¦27660 oppression.
\v 8 Then¦27662 I'll¦27662 \add take\add* you¦27663 all to¦27664 the¦27666 land¦27666 that¦27667 I¦27668 \add promised\add* to¦27672 give¦27672 to¦27674 Abraham¦27674, to¦27675 Yitshak¦27675, and¦27676 to¦27676 Yacob¦27676,\x + \xo 6:8: \xt Gen 15:18; 26:3; 28:13.\x* and¦27677 I'll¦27677 give¦27677 it¦27678 to¦27679 you¦27679 as \add your own\add*. I¦27681 am¦27682 Yahweh¦27682.’ ”
\v 9 \add ≈So¦27684\add* Mosheh¦27685 \add passed all that on\add* to¦27687 the¦27689 Israelis¦27690, but¦27691 they¦27692 wouldn't listen¦27692 to¦27693 Mosheh¦27695, because they were \add both discouraged and worn out from the slavery\add*.
\p
\v 10 Then¦27702 Yahweh¦27703 told¦27702 Mosheh¦27706,
\v 11 “Go¦27709 and speak¦27710 to¦27711 the¦27714 Egyptian¦27715 king¦27714 Far'oh¦27713 and¦27716 \add tell him\add* that he¦27716 needs to let¦27716 the¦27719 Israelis¦27721 leave his¦27722 country¦27722.”
\v 12 And¦27724 Mosheh¦27725 said¦27724 in¦27726 front¦27726 of¦27726 Yahweh¦27727, “Look, the¦27730 Israelis¦27732 haven't listened¦27735 to¦27736 me¦27736, so¦27737 why would¦27738 Far'oh¦27739 listen¦27738? And¦27740 I¦27740 have \add difficulty speaking\add*.”
\rem /s1 Family Record of Moses and Aaron
\v 13 Then¦27745 Yahweh¦27746 spoke¦27745 to¦27747 Mosheh¦27749 and¦27750 Aharon¦27752, and¦27753 he¦27753 gave them¦27753 a command¦27753 to¦27754 \add pass on to\add* the¦27756 Israelis¦27757 and¦27758 to¦27758 the¦27761 Egyptian¦27762 king¦27761 Far'oh¦27760: to¦27763 \add take\add* the¦27766 Israelis¦27768 out¦27763 of¦27769 Egypt¦27770.
\s1 The ancestors of Mosheh and Aharon
\rem /s1 The Ancestors of Moses and Aaron; The Family Record of Moses and Aaron; The Genealogy of Moses and Aaron
\p
\v 14 The following were¦27774 the¦27774 leaders of¦27774 the¦27775 clans:
\p The¦27778 sons¦27778 of¦27778 \bd Reuben¦27779\bd* (Yisra'el's¦27781 eldest¦27780 son) were¦27782 Hanoch¦27782, Pallu¦27783, Hezron¦27784, and¦27785 Karmi¦27785, and \add they headed\add* the¦27787 clans¦27787 of¦27787 Reuben¦27788.
\p
\v 15 \bd Simeon¦27791\bd*'s sons¦27790 were¦27792 Yemuel, Yamin¦27793, Ohad¦27794, Yakin¦27795, Zohar¦27796, and¦27797 Shaul¦27797—the¦27798 son¦27798 of¦27798 a Canaanite¦27800 \add >woman\add*, and \add they headed\add* the¦27802 clans¦27802 of¦27802 Simeon¦27803.
\p
\v 16 \bd Levi¦27809\bd*'s sons¦27807 were Gershon¦27811, Kohath¦27812, and¦27813 Merari¦27813.\x + \xo 6:16-19: \xt Num 3:17-20; 26:57-58; 1Ch 6:16-19.\x* Levi¦27816 was¦27817 137 years¦27820 old when he died.
\v 17 Gershon's¦27823 sons¦27822 were¦27824 Libni¦27824 and¦27825 Shimei¦27825, and \add they headed\add* their¦27826 clans¦27826.
\v 18 Kohath's¦27829 sons¦27828 were¦27830 Amram¦27830, Izhar¦27831, Hebron¦27832, and¦27833 Uzziel¦27833. Kohath¦27836 was¦27837 133 years¦27840 old when he died.
\v 19 Merari's¦27843 sons¦27842 were¦27844 Mahli¦27844 and¦27845 Mushi¦27845, and \add all of those headed\add* the¦27847 clans¦27847 of¦27847 Levi.
\v 20 Amram¦27852 married his aunt Yochebed¦27855, and¦27859 she¦27859 gave¦27859 birth¦27859 to¦27860 Aharon¦27863 and¦27864 Mosheh¦27866. Amram¦27869 \add ≈lived¦27868\add* to be¦27870 137 years¦27873 old.
\v 21 Izhar's¦27876 sons¦27875 were¦27877 Korah¦27877, Nefeg¦27878, and¦27879 Zichri¦27879.
\v 22 Uzziel's¦27882 sons¦27881 were¦27883 Mishael¦27883, Elzafan¦27884, and¦27885 Sithri¦27885.
