\v 3 along with \add #their\add* two¦33907 sons¦33908.\x + \xo 18:3: \xt Acts 7:29.\x* One¦33911 was named¦33910 ‘Gershom¦33912’ \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘foreigner’)\add* because¦33913 \add @Mosheh¦33914\add* had¦33914 said¦33914, “I've¦33916 been a¦33915 foreigner¦33915 living in¦33917 another country¦33917.”
\v 4 \add Their\add* other son's name¦33920 was¦33922 ‘Eliezer¦33922’ \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word that means ‘God helps me’\add*, because¦33923 Mosheh had said, “God¦33925, who my¦33926 father¦33926 worshipped, has¦33927 helped¦33927 me and \add ≈stopped the¦33929 Egyptian king Far'oh¦33930 from killing me\add*.”))
\p
\v 5 \add ≈So¦33932\add* \add now\add* Mosheh's¦33935 father-in-law¦33934 brought \add @Mosheh's\add* wife¦33937 and sons¦33936 to¦33938 the¦33943 wilderness¦33943 where¦33944 \add #the people\add* were¦33947 camping¦33947 \add near\add* the¦33949 mountain¦33949 of¦33949 God.
\v 6 \add ≈He¦33952 had sent a message ahead\add* saying¦33952, “I¦33956, your¦33957 father-in-law¦33957 Yetro, am¦33959 coming¦33959 to¦33960 you¦33960, as well as your¦33961 wife¦33961 and¦33962 \add %your\add* two¦33962 sons¦33963.”
\v 7 \add ≈So¦33966\add* Mosheh¦33967 went¦33966 out¦33966 to¦33968 meet¦33968 his¦33969 father-in-law¦33969, and¦33970 he¦33970 bowed¦33970 down¦33970 and¦33971 kissed¦33971 him¦33973. They¦33974 asked¦33974 each¦33975 other about¦33977 their welfare¦33978, then¦33979 they¦33979 went¦33979 into¦33980 the¦33980 tent¦33980,
\v 8 and¦33982 Mosheh¦33983 related to¦33984 his¦33984 father-in-law¦33984 all¦33986 that¦33988 Yahweh¦33990 had¦33989 done¦33989 to¦33991 Far'oh¦33991 and¦33992 to¦33992 Egypt¦33992 on¦33993 account of¦33994 Yisrael¦33995, all¦33997 the¦33999 hardship¦33999 that¦34000 found them¦34001 along the way¦34002, and¦34003 about how Yahweh had¦34003 rescued¦34003 them¦34003.
\v 9 Yetro \add ≈was very happy to hear about\add* all¦34009 the¦34011 good¦34011 \add things\add* that¦34012 Yahweh¦34015 had¦34014 done¦34014 for Yisrael¦34016 when¦34006 he¦34018 had¦34018 rescued¦34018 them out from¦34019 Egyptian¦34020 \add ≈control\add*,
\v 10 and¦34022 \add *he¦34023\add* said¦34022, “Bless¦34024 Yahweh¦34025, who¦34026 rescued¦34027 you¦34028 from¦34029 \add slavery\add* in Egypt¦34030, and¦34031 from¦34031 Far'oh's¦34032 \add ≈power\add*.
\v 11 Now¦34043 I¦34044 \add ≈can see¦34044\add* that¦34045 Yahweh¦34048 is¦34047 greater¦34047 than¦34049 all¦34049 the¦34051 \add other\add* gods because¦34052 of the \add this result after\add* \add @the¦34055 Egyptians¦34055\add* had¦34055 acted¦34055 proudly against \add @you¦34056 all¦34056\add*.”
\v 12 Then¦34058 Mosheh's¦34061 father-in-law Yetro made a¦34062 burnt¦34062 offering¦34062 as well as sacrifices¦34063 to¦34064 God¦34064, and¦34065 Aharon¦34066 and¦34067 all¦34067 of the¦34069 Israeli¦34070 elders¦34069 came¦34065 to¦34071 eat¦34071 bread¦34073 with¦34074 Mosheh's¦34077 father-in-law \add ≈in¦34078 God's presence¦34078\add*.
\s1 Yetro's helpful administrative advice
\rem /s1 Jethro's Wise Advice; The Appointment of Judges
\r (Deu. 1:9-18)
\p
\v 13 The¦34082 following¦34082 day¦34082, Mosheh¦34084 sat¦34083 down¦34083 to¦34085 judge¦34085 the¦34088 people¦34088, and¦34089 the¦34090 people¦34090 stood¦34089 around \add *him¦34093\add* from¦34094 morning¦34096 until¦34097 evening¦34099.
\v 14 When¦34101 \add *his\add* father-in-law¦34102 noticed¦34101 everything that¦34107 he¦34109 \add personally\add* did¦34110 for¦34111 the people¦34111, he¦34112 \add asked\add*, “What's¦34113 this¦34116 that¦34117 you're¦34118 doing¦34119 with the people¦34120? Why¦34121 do you¦34122 sit¦34123 \add there\add* alone¦34124, and¦34125 all¦34125 the¦34127 people¦34127 position themselves around you¦34129 from¦34130 morning¦34132 until¦34133 evening¦34135?”
\p
\v 15 ⇔“Because¦34140 the¦34144 people¦34144 come¦34142 to¦34143 me¦34143 to¦34145 \add ≈find out what God¦34146 wants for them\add*,” Mosheh¦34138 replied¦34137 to¦34139 \add @Yetro\add*.
\v 16 “When a¦34152 \add difficult\add* situation¦34152 comes up among them, someone comes to¦34154 me¦34154 and¦34155 I¦34155 make¦34160 the \add ≈judgement¦34155\add* between¦34156 a man and¦34158 his¦34159 neighbour¦34159. \add In addition,\add* I help them understand the¦34163 god's statutes¦34163 and¦34165 laws¦34167.”
\p
\v 17 “It's not¦34173 a good¦34175 \add idea\add* what you're¦34178 doing¦34179,” said¦34169 Yetro.
\v 18 “You're definitely \add on the road to\add* wear¦34181 yourself out¦34181, plus you'll¦34182 also wear¦34182 out¦34182 these people¦34188 who are¦34191 with¦34191 you¦34191, because¦34192 the \add responsibility\add* is¦34194 too¦34194 heavy¦34194 for¦34195 you¦34195—you¦34199 can't¦34197 \add just\add* do¦34200 it¦34200 alone¦34201.
\v 19 Now¦34203, listen¦34204 to¦34205 \add this\add*: I'll advise¦34206 you¦34206 and¦34207 God will be with¦34209 you. You be before God for¦34212 the people¦34212, and¦34215 you¦34215 should bring¦34215 their disputes to¦34220 God yourself.
\v 20 You¦34224 should \add also\add* teach them¦34225 the¦34228 statutes¦34228 and¦34229 the¦34231 laws¦34231, and¦34232 you¦34232 should \add teach\add* them¦34233 the¦34236 way¦34236 they¦34237 should \add live\add* and¦34239 the¦34241 work¦34241 that¦34242 they¦34243 should do¦34243.
\v 21 \add But\add* you yourself should search through all¦34247 the¦34249 people¦34249 to find capable men who¦34257 are godly and \add ≈faithful¦34256\add*, and not \add greedy for\add* dishonest¦34258 profit¦34258. Then¦34259 appoint¦34259 \add the best of them\add* to be over¦34260 a thousand¦34262 \add >households\add*, and then others to be over groups of¦34263 one hundred¦34264, fifty¦34266, and¦34267 ten¦34268 \add >households\add*.
\v 22 They'll¦34270 \add be the ones to\add* judge¦34270 the¦34273 people¦34273, except that every¦34278 serious situation¦34280 they'll¦34282 bring¦34282 to¦34283 you¦34283—they'll¦34288 judge¦34288 every minor situation themselves. \add Yes,\add* you must lighten¦34291 this load off yourself¦34292, and¦34293 they'll¦34293 share the load with¦34294 you¦34294.
\v 23 If¦34296 you¦34301 do¦34301 that, and¦34302 if God¦34303 tells you \add to proceed\add*, then¦34304 you'll¦34304 be¦34304 able¦34304 to¦34305 endure¦34305 \add in the long term\add*, plus¦34306 all¦34307 of¦34307 these people¦34309 \add standing around\add* will be able to return¦34314 to their tents peacefully¦34315.”
\p
\v 24 \add ≈So¦34317\add* Mosheh¦34318 \add ≈took his father-in-law's¦34320 advice\add* and¦34321 \add ≈implemented all¦34322 of his suggestions\add*.
\v 25 \add *He¦34327\add* chose¦34326 capable men¦34328 from¦34331 among all¦34331 the Israelis¦34333, and¦34334 he¦34334 appointed them¦34335 as heads¦34336 over¦34337 the¦34339 people¦34339: leaders¦34340 of¦34340 thousands¦34341, leaders¦34342 of¦34342 hundreds¦34343, leaders¦34344 of¦34344 fifties¦34345, and¦34346 leaders¦34346 of¦34346 tens¦34347 \add of households\add*.
\v 26 They¦34349 became the ones who settled most disputes for the¦34352 people¦34352. They¦34360 brought¦34360 the¦34359 difficult¦34359 cases to¦34361 Mosheh¦34363, \add but\add* they¦34368 judged¦34368 each minor situation themselves.
\p
\v 27 Then¦34371 Mosheh¦34372 bid farewell to \add @Yetro\add* and¦34376 he¦34376 returned¦34376 to his¦34380 own home.
\c 19
\s1 Camping by Mt. Sinai
\rem /s1 At Mount Sinai; The Lord Reveals Himself at Sinai; The Israelites at Mount Sinai; The Israelites Reach Mount Sinai; Israel at Mount Sinai
\p
\v 1 ⇔The¦34386 Israelis¦34388 entered the Sinai¦34395 wilderness¦34394 in the¦34384 third¦34384 month¦34383 after exiting¦34385 Egypt¦34390.
\v 2 \add After\add* they¦34397 had departed¦34397 from¦34398 Refidim¦34398, they¦34399 had entered the¦34400 Sinai¦34401 wilderness¦34403 and¦34404 camped¦34404 there¦34406 \add ≈at the base\add* of the¦34409 mountain¦34409.
\v 3 Yahweh¦34418 called¦34416 Mosheh¦34411 from¦34419 the¦34421 mountain¦34421, so¦34423 he¦34412 climbed up¦34412 and God gave him this message and asked him to pass it on to Yacob's¦34426 descendants, the¦34428 Israelis¦34429:
\p
\v 4 “You yourselves saw¦34432 what I¦34434 did¦34434 to¦34435 the Egyptians—how I¦34436 carried¦34436 you¦34437 on¦34438 eagles¦34441' wings¦34440 and¦34442 brought¦34442 you¦34443 all \add here\add* to¦34444 myself¦34444.
\v 5 And¦34446 now¦34446, if¦34447 you all carefully¦34449 listen to¦34451 my¦34451 \add ≈words¦34451\add* and¦34452 keep¦34452 my¦34455 agreement¦34455, then¦34456 \add ≈your nation\add* will¦34456 be¦34456 my possession¦34458, \add separate\add* from¦34459 all¦34459 the¦34461 \add others\add*,\x + \xo 19:5-6: \xt 1Pe 2:9.\x* because¦34462 all¦34465 the¦34467 earth¦34467 is mine,
\v 6 \add ≈So¦34469\add* you all will¦34470 be¦34470 my kingdom¦34473 of¦34473 priests¦34474 and¦34475 a¦34475 nation¦34475 \add dedicated to me\add*.”\x + \xo 19:6: \xt Rev 1:6; 5:10.\x* (That's the¦34478 \add #message¦34478\add* to pass on to the¦34483 Israelis¦34484.)
\v 7 \add ≈So¦34486\add* Mosheh¦34487 descended \add again\add* and¦34488 summoned¦34488 the¦34489 Israeli elders¦34489, then¦34491 he¦34491 passed¦34486 on that¦34498 \add #message¦34496\add* \add just\add* as Yahweh¦34500 had¦34499 commanded¦34499 him¦34499,
\v 8 and¦34502 all¦34503 the¦34505 people¦34505 responded¦34502 together¦34506, \add agreeing\add*, “We'll¦34513 do everything that¦34509 Yahweh¦34512 has¦34511 said¦34511.” Then¦34514 Mosheh¦34515 took¦34514 the¦34519 people's¦34519 response back¦34514 to¦34520 Yahweh¦34522
\v 9 and¦34524 Yahweh¦34525 told¦34524 \add *him¦34528\add*, “Look, I'll¦34530 come¦34531 to¦34532 you¦34532 in¦34533 a thick cloud¦34533 so that the¦34534 people¦34537 will be able to hear¦34536 when¦34538 I speak¦34538 with¦34539 you \add so they'll also continue to\add* trust¦34543 you.”
\rem /s1 The People Consecrated
\p Then¦34545 Mosheh¦34546 told¦34545 Yahweh the¦34549 people's words¦34549
\v 10 and¦34555 Yahweh¦34556 \add ≈responded¦34555\add*, “Go¦34560 \add down\add* to¦34561 the¦34563 people¦34563 and¦34564 purify them¦34564 today and¦34566 tomorrow¦34566. Have them wash¦34567 their¦34568 clothes¦34568
\v 11 and¦34570 be¦34570 prepared¦34571 for¦34572 the¦34573 third¦34573 day, because¦34574 on the third day \add *I¦34579\add* will¦34578 come¦34578 down¦34578 to¦34580 Mt¦34586. Sinai¦34587 while all¦34581 the¦34583 people¦34583 watch.
\v 12 Set¦34589 boundaries¦34589 for¦34589 the¦34592 people¦34592 all¦34593 around¦34593, and tell¦34594 \add <them\add*, ‘Don't climb¦34597 on¦34598 the mountain¦34598 or touch¦34599 its¦34600 edge¦34600, because anyone¦34603 who¦34603 does will¦34606 definitely be¦34606 executed¦34606.\x + \xo 19:12-13: \xt Heb 12:18-20.\x*
\v 13 Anyone \add who touches the mountain\add* mustn't be touched¦34610 by anyone else—they must¦34616 be¦34616 \add ≈executed¦34616 by throwing rocks at them\add* or¦34617 shooting them \add with a bow\add*. No person or animal¦34623 \add who touches the mountain\add* can stay alive¦34628.’ \add ≈Only after a¦34626 long trumpet¦34630 blast¦34629\add* should they \add start to\add* climb¦34632 the mountain¦34633.”
\v 14 \add ≈So¦34635\add* Mosheh¦34636 went down the¦34639 mountain¦34639 to¦34640 the¦34642 people¦34642. He¦34643 purified them and¦34647 they¦34647 \add all\add* washed¦34647 their¦34648 clothes¦34648,
\v 15 and¦34650 he¦34650 told¦34650 \add *them\add*, “Abstain from sexual relations, and be¦34654 ready¦34655 by the third day¦34657.”
\p
\v 16 \add ≈So¦34665\add* on¦34666 the¦34669 morning¦34669 of the¦34667 third¦34667 day¦34666, there was¦34670 thunder¦34671 and¦34672 lightning¦34672\x + \xo 19:16: \xt Rev 4:5.\x* and¦34673 a¦34673 thick cloud¦34673 on¦34675 the¦34677 mountain¦34677, and¦34678 an extremely loud¦34678 horn¦34679 blast, and¦34682 all¦34683 the¦34685 people¦34685 in¦34687 the camp¦34687 were trembling¦34682.
\v 17 Then¦34689 Mosheh¦34690 \add ≈led¦34689\add* the¦34693 people¦34693 out¦34689 of the¦34698 camp¦34698 to \add go and\add* meet¦34694 God, and¦34699 they¦34699 stationed themselves at¦34700 the¦34700 \add ≈foot\add* of¦34700 the¦34701 mountain¦34701.
\v 18 Yahweh¦34711 descended¦34709 onto Mt. Sinai¦34704 \add surrounded\add* by fire¦34712 and¦34703 the¦34703 entire mountain¦34703 smoked¦34705. Its¦34706 smoke¦34714 went up like¦34715 the¦34715 smoke¦34715 from a furnace¦34716, and¦34717 the¦34720 whole mountain¦34720 trembled¦34717 violently.
\v 19 Then¦34723 it happened \add after\add* the¦34724 sound¦34724 of¦34724 the¦34725 horn¦34725 got extremely loud, that Mosheh¦34729 was speaking¦34730 and¦34731 God¦34731 was answering¦34732 him¦34732 in a¦34733 voice¦34733.
\v 20 Yahweh¦34736 had descended¦34735 onto the¦34743 top¦34743 of¦34743 Mt¦34744. Sinai¦34740, and¦34745 \add *he¦34746\add* called¦34745 Mosheh¦34747 up to¦34748 the¦34750 top¦34750 of¦34750 the¦34751 mountain¦34751, \add so\add* Mosheh¦34753 ascended¦34752.
\v 21 \add But\add* Yahweh¦34756 told¦34755 \add *him¦34759\add*, “Go¦34760 \add back\add* down¦34760 and warn¦34761 the people¦34762 in case they¦34765 force their way through¦34765 to¦34766 look¦34769 at \add *me¦34768\add* and¦34770 many¦34772 of them perish.
\v 22 Also¦34774, the¦34775 priests¦34775 who¦34776 come¦34776 nearer to¦34777 \add *me¦34779\add*, they¦34780 \add ≈must¦34780 be¦34784 very careful to purify themselves¦34780\add* in¦34784 case \add *I\add* \add ≈suddenly punish\add* them¦34784.”
\v 23 \add But\add* Mosheh¦34788 \add questioned\add* Yahweh¦34791, “The¦34795 people¦34795 aren't¦34792 able¦34794 to¦34796 come¦34796 up¦34796 on Mt¦34799. Sinai¦34800, because¦34801 you yourself warned¦34804 us¦34805, ‘Set¦34807 boundaries¦34807 all around the¦34810 mountain¦34810 to keep it¦34811 sacred.’ ”\x + \xo 19:23 \xt 19:12.\x*
\v 24 “Go \add back\add* down¦34818,” Yahweh \add ≈responded¦34813\add*, “and bring Aharon¦34821 back up with¦34822 you¦34822. \add However,\add* the¦34823 priests¦34823 and¦34824 the¦34824 people¦34824 \add ≈must not¦34825 cross the boundary\add* to¦34828 come¦34828 up¦34828 to¦34829 \add *me\add*, in case \add *I\add* \add ≈suddenly punish\add* them¦34836.”
\v 25 \add ≈So¦34838\add* Mosheh¦34839 went down and¦34843 spoke¦34843 to¦34840 the¦34842 people¦34842.
\c 20
\s1 The ten rules
\rem /s1 The Ten Commandments
\r (Deu. 5:1-22)
\p
\v 1 Then¦34847 God¦34848 \add ≈gave these¦34853 instructions\add* \add to the Israelis\add*:
\v 2 I'm¦34857 your¦34860 god¦34860 Yahweh¦34859, who¦34862 \add ≈rescued you¦34863\add*\f + \fr 20:2 \ft Although modern English doesn't easily distinguish it, this discourse is addressed to singular ‘you’, i.e., to the Israelis as one group. While some of our modern, individualistic cultures might naturally interpret these rules as applying to individuals, they were given to Yisrael/Israel to implement at a national level.\f* \add ≈from¦34864 where you were enslaved in\add* Egypt¦34865.
\li1
\v 3 You mustn't own any other¦34879 gods¦34877 \add ≈in my¦34883 sight\add*.
\li1
\v 4 You¦34888 mustn't make¦34888 a carved figure for¦34890 yourself¦34890, nor \add carve a copy\add* of anything in¦34901 the sky¦34901 above¦34903 or in the¦34907 earth¦34907 below¦34909, or that's¦34912 in¦34914 the \add #water¦34914\add* under¦34916 the earth¦34918.\x + \xo 20:4-5: \xt Exo 34:17; Lev 19:4; 26:1; Deu 4:15-18; 27:15.\x*
\v 5 Don't¦34922 bow¦34924 down¦34924 to¦34927 \add @idols¦34927\add* and¦34929 don't¦34929 serve¦34930 them¦34930, because¦34932 I¦34933, your god Yahweh¦34934, am¦34936 a¦34936 jealous¦34937 God. I remember the¦34939 sins of¦34939 the fathers¦34940 and \add punish\add* the children¦34943 of \add even\add* the third¦34946 and¦34947 fourth¦34949 \add generation\add* of¦34950 those¦34950 who¦34950 hate¦34950 me¦34950,\x + \xo 20:5-6: \xt Exo 34:6-7; Num 14:18; Deu 7:9-10.\x*
\v 6 \add but\add* I \add ≈faithfully fulfil my promises\add* to¦34957 \em thousands¦34957\em* \add of generations\add* of¦34959 those¦34959 who¦34959 love¦34959 me¦34959 and¦34960 who¦34960 obey my¦34961 instructions¦34961.
\li1
\v 7 You¦34965 mustn't \add ≈wrongly promote\add* the¦34968 name¦34968 of¦34968 Yahweh¦34970 your¦34971 god¦34971, because¦34973 \add *I¦34976\add* won't leave¦34975 anyone unpunished¦34975 who¦34978 \add ≈does that\add*.\x + \xo 20:7: \xt Lev 19:12.\x*
\li1
\v 8 \add ≈Keep Saturday different from all the¦34991 other days¦34991\add*\x + \xo 20:8: \xt Exo 16:23-30; 31:12-14.\x*—
\v 9 there's six¦34999 days¦35001 \add every week\add* for working¦35009.\x + \xo 20:9-10: \xt Exo 23:12; 31:15; 34:21; 35:2; Lev 23:3.\x*
\v 10 The¦35014 seventh¦35014 day is¦35016 a¦35016 rest day for¦35018 Yahweh¦35018 your¦35019 god¦35019: you¦35023 mustn't do¦35023 any¦35025 work¦35027—not you¦35029, or your children¦35031, or your¦35035 male¦35035 or female¦35037 servants¦35037, or your¦35039 cattle¦35039, or the foreigners¦35041 \add ≈living among you\add*—
\v 11 because¦35049 Yahweh¦35054 made¦35053 the¦35057 heavens¦35057 and¦35058 earth¦35060, the¦35063 sea¦35063, and¦35064 everything that's¦35068 in¦35070 them¦35070 in six¦35050 days¦35052. Then¦35071 he¦35071 rested¦35071 on the¦35073 seventh¦35073 day, so¦35076 that's why \add *he¦35078\add* blessed¦35077 the¦35081 rest day¦35081 and¦35083 made it¦35083 sacred¦35083.\x + \xo 20:11: \xt Gen 2:1-3; Exo 31:17.\x*
\li1
\v 12 Honour¦35086 your¦35089 father¦35089 and¦35090 your¦35092 mother¦35092,\x + \xo 20:12: \xt Deu 27:16; Mat 15:4; 19:19; Mrk 7:10; 10:19; Luk 18:20; Eph 6:2.\x* so¦35093 that¦35093 \add ≈you'll have a long¦35094 life\add* on¦35096 the¦35097 land¦35097 that¦35098 your¦35101 god¦35101 Yahweh¦35100 is¦35102 about¦35102 to¦35102 give¦35102 you¦35103.\x + \xo 20:12: \xt Eph 6:3.\x*
\li1
\v 13 You¦35108 mustn't murder¦35108.\x + \xo 20:13: \xt Gen 9:6; Lev 24:17; Mat 5:21; 19:18; Mrk 10:19; Luk 18:20; Rom 13:9; Jam 2:11.\x*
\li1
\v 14 You¦35114 mustn't commit¦35114 adultery¦35114.\x + \xo 20:14: \xt Lev 20:10; Mat 5:27; 19:18; Mrk 10:19; Luk 18:20; Rom 13:9; Jam 2:11.\x*
\li1
\v 15 You¦35120 mustn't steal¦35120.\x + \xo 20:15: \xt Lev 19:11; Mat 19:18; Mrk 10:19; Luk 18:20; Rom 13:9.\x*
\li1
\v 16 You¦35126 mustn't \add ≈lie in court\add*.\x + \xo 20:16: \xt Exo 23:1; Mat 19:18; Mrk 10:19; Luk 18:20.\x*
\li1
\v 17 You¦35133 mustn't covet¦35133 your neighbour's house¦35134 or spouse, or their male¦35141 or female¦35142 servants¦35142, or their animals, or anything else they own.\x + \xo 20:17: \xt Rom 7:7; 13:9.\x*
\s1 The people are afraid of God
\rem /s1 The People's Fear
\r (Deu. 5:22-33)
\p
\v 18 All¦35150 of the¦35152 people¦35152 could hear and see¦35153 the¦35156 thunder¦35156 and¦35157 the¦35159 lightning¦35159, the¦35161 sound¦35161 of¦35161 the¦35162 horn¦35162, and¦35163 the¦35165 mountain¦35165 smoking¦35166. They¦35169 trembled¦35169 \add when\add* \add *they\add* saw¦35167 it, and¦35170 they¦35170 \add remained\add* standing¦35170 at a¦35171 distance¦35171.\x + \xo 20:18-19: \xt Heb 12:18-19.\x*
\v 19 They¦35173 \add begged\add* Mosheh¦35176, “You¦35179 be the one to speak¦35177 to us¦35180, and¦35181 we'll¦35181 listen¦35181, but¦35182 don't¦35182 let¦35184 God¦35186 speak¦35184 \add directly\add* to us¦35185, in case we¦35189 die¦35189.”
\p
\v 20 “Don't¦35196 be¦35198 afraid¦35198,” Mosheh¦35192 responded¦35191, “because¦35199 God has¦35203 come¦35203 to test¦35201 you¦35202 so¦35205 that¦35205 the fear¦35207 of¦35207 him \add ≈will¦35212 remain with you\add* so that you all won't \add ≈disobey him\add*.”
\v 21 \add ≈So¦35214\add* the¦35215 people¦35215 stood¦35214 \add ≈at a¦35216 good distance¦35216\add* \add while\add* Mosheh¦35217 approached¦35218 toward the¦35221 thick¦35221 darkness¦35221 where¦35222 God was¦35224.
\s1 Instructions about altars
\rem /s1 The Law concerning the Altar; Proper Use of Altars; Idols and Altars; Laws about Altars
\p
\v 22 Then¦35228 Yahweh¦35229 told¦35228 Mosheh¦35232, “This is what you¦35234 need to¦35235 tell the¦35237 Israelis¦35238: ‘You yourselves have¦35240 seen¦35240 that¦35241 I¦35245, \add Yahweh,\add* have¦35245 talked¦35245 with¦35246 you¦35246 from \add #heaven¦35244\add*.
\v 23 You¦35249 all mustn't make¦35249 gods¦35251 out of¦35251 silver¦35252 or gold¦35254 for¦35257 yourselves¦35257 to have alongside me.
\v 24 You¦35261 need to make¦35261 an¦35259 altar¦35259 for¦35263 me¦35263 out of dirt, and¦35264 then you¦35264 can sacrifice¦35264 your¦35268 burnt¦35268 offerings and¦35269 peace¦35271 offerings¦35271, and your¦35271 sheep¦35274 and¦35275 cattle¦35277, on it. \add ≈I'll inform you about¦35277 every¦35278 place¦35280 where¦35281 I¦35282 want to¦35282 be¦35282 worshipped\add* and I'll¦35286 come¦35286 to¦35287 you¦35287 and¦35288 will¦35288 bless¦35288 you¦35288 \add there\add*.
\v 25 If¦35290 you¦35294 do make¦35294 me an¦35292 altar¦35292 with stones¦35293, you mustn't use cut stones to¦35296 build¦35299 it because¦35302 moving your¦35303 \add #tools¦35303\add* over it will defile it.\x + \xo 20:25: \xt Deu 27:5-7; Josh 8:31.\x*
\v 26 And¦35308 don't¦35308 use steps¦35311 to get up¦35310 to¦35312 the altar¦35314 because you might expose¦35318 your¦35319 \add ≈private parts\add* \add under your robes\add*.
\c 21
\s1 Rules about slaves
\rem /s1 The Law concerning Slaves; The Treatment of Slaves; Fair Treatment of Slaves
\r (Deu. 15:12-18)
\p
\v 1 These¦35323 are¦35324 the¦35324 rules that¦35325 you¦35326 must¦35326 present to them¦35327:
\s2 Hebrew slaves
\rem /s1 Hebrew Servants
\p
\v 2 If¦35329 you¦35330 buy¦35330 a¦35331 Hebrew¦35332 slave¦35331, he¦35335 should serve¦35335 for six¦35333 years¦35334, then¦35336 in¦35336 the seventh year¦35336 he¦35337 must¦35337 be \add ≈set free\add* without¦35339 having to¦35338 pay¦35339 you anything.\x + \xo 21:2-6: \xt Lev 25:39-46.\x*
\v 3 If¦35341 he¦35344 came¦35344 by himself, he¦35346 can go¦35346 free by himself. If¦35347 he's married with a¦35350 wife¦35350, then¦35352 his¦35353 wife¦35353 must be \add ≈set free\add* with¦35354 him¦35354.
\v 4 If¦35356 his¦35358 master gave¦35359 him¦35361 a¦35362 wife¦35362 and¦35363 she¦35363 bore¦35363 him¦35365 sons¦35366 or¦35367 daughters¦35368, then the¦35369 wife¦35369 and¦35370 her¦35372 children¦35370 are her¦35370 master's¦35372, and¦35373 only the husband must¦35374 be \add ≈set free\add*.
\v 5 \add But\add* if¦35377 \add *he\add* plainly says, “I¦35382 love¦35382 my¦35385 master¦35385, my¦35388 wife¦35388, and¦35389 my¦35391 children¦35391 so I \add voluntarily\add* choose not¦35392 to be \add ≈set free¦35394\add*,”
\v 6 then¦35396 his master must¦35396 take¦35396 him¦35396 to¦35398 \add ?≈the¦35400 place of worship\add*. He¦35401 must¦35401 bring¦35401 him¦35401 to¦35402 a door¦35404 or¦35405 to¦35406 a doorpost¦35408, and¦35409 his master¦35410 must bore a hole his¦35410 ear¦35413 with¦35414 an awl¦35414. Then¦35415 he¦35415 must¦35415 serve¦35415 him¦35415 \add for life\add*.
\p
\v 7 And¦35419 if a¦35422 man¦35422 sells¦35421 his¦35425 daughter¦35425 as a¦35426 female¦35426 slave¦35426, she¦35428 \add ≈won't¦35427 be sent away\add* \add after six years\add* like¦35429 the¦35430 male¦35430 slaves¦35430.
\v 8 If¦35432 the man who¦35437 bought her¦35436 \add ≈wanted her¦35436 to become his slave-wife\add*, but \add later\add* \add ≈he¦35441 isn't¦35445 pleased with her¦35441 behaviour\add*, he¦35442 must¦35442 allow¦35442 her¦35442 to be¦35442 \add ≈bought back\add* by her \add ≈relatives\add*. He¦35447 has¦35447 no authority to¦35448 sell¦35448 her¦35448 on to outsiders, because¦35449 that would \add ≈break the understanding of proper treatment\add* of her¦35451.
\v 9 Or if¦35453 he selected her \add ≈to become his son's¦35455 slave-wife\add*, then¦35453 he¦35456 must¦35456 treat her¦35456 \add ≈like a daughter¦35458 would¦35459 expect to¦35461 be treated\add*.
\v 10 If¦35463 he¦35466 \add ≈go on to take¦35466 another¦35465 slave-wife\add*, he¦35473 mustn't reduce¦35473 \add @the¦35468 first¦35468 one's¦35468\add* food¦35469 or clothing¦35470, or her marital \add #rights¦35471\add*,
\v 11 \add but\add* if¦35475 he doesn't provide these¦35479 three¦35477 \add >things\add* to¦35482 her¦35482, then¦35483 she¦35483 can go¦35483 free without¦35484 \add ≈having to pay¦35484 anything\add*.
\s1 Rules about violence to others
\rem /s1 The Law concerning Violence; Laws about Violent Acts; Cases of Personal Injury; Personal Injuries
\p
\v 12 Anyone¦35489 who¦35489 strikes¦35489 someone¦35490 and¦35491 that person dies, must¦35493 definitely be¦35493 put¦35493 to¦35493 death¦35493.\x + \xo 21:12: \xt Lev 24:17.\x*
\v 13 But if \add ≈it wasn't premeditated\add*—if God¦35498 had let¦35499 it happen¦35499 as an accident—then¦35501 I'll¦35501 assign¦35501 a¦35503 place¦35503 where¦35504 \add @the¦35505 one¦35505 who¦35505 hit¦35505 them¦35505\add* can flee¦35505 to¦35506.\x + \xo 21:13: \xt Num 35:10-34; Deu 19:1-13; Josh 20:1-9.\x*
\v 14 \add However,\add* if someone¦35512 \add ≈had schemed to¦35516 trap and\add* kill¦35516 their neighbour¦35515, then \add even if the killer clings to\add* my¦35519 altar¦35519, you¦35520 must¦35520 take¦35520 them for execution.
\p
\v 15 Anyone¦35524 who¦35524 strikes¦35524 their father¦35525 or mother¦35526 must certainly¦35527 be¦35528 put¦35528 to¦35528 death¦35528.
\p
\v 16 Anyone¦35530 who¦35530 abducts another person—either to sell¦35532 or to keep \add as a slave\add*—\add @that person\add* must¦35533 certainly¦35535 be¦35536 put¦35536 to¦35536 death¦35536.\x + \xo 21:16: \xt Deu 24:7.\x*
\p
\v 17 Anyone¦35539 who¦35539 curses¦35539 their father¦35540 or mother¦35541 must certainly¦35542 be¦35543 put¦35543 to¦35543 death¦35543.\x + \xo 21:17: \xt Lev 20:9; Mat 15:4; Mrk 7:10.\x*
\p
\v 18 If two people¦35549 fight and¦35550 one hits¦35550 their neighbour¦35555 with¦35556 a¦35556 stone¦35556 or¦35557 with¦35558 \add &their¦35558\add* fist¦35558, and¦35559 the injured one doesn't die¦35560 but¦35561 is bedridden,
\v 19 if¦35564 that person \add eventually\add* rises¦35566 and¦35567 can walk¦35567 about¦35567 outside¦35568 using a staff¦35571, then¦35572 the¦35573 person who¦35573 struck¦35573 them is¦35572 free¦35572, other than¦35572 \add ≈reimbursing the costs of¦35575 their lost work and¦35577 their medical expenses\add*.
\p
\v 20 If a master hits¦35583 their male¦35587 or¦35588 female¦35591 slave with¦35592 a staff¦35592 and¦35593 \add @the¦35593 slave¦35593\add* dies¦35593, then the death must certainly¦35596 be¦35597 avenged¦35597.
\v 21 However¦35599, if¦35600 \add @the slave\add* is able to stand within a¦35602 couple of days¦35604, then the \add owner\add* won't¦35606 be¦35607 \add ≈punished\add* because¦35608 \add @the slave\add* is their property¦35609.
\p
\v 22 If two people¦35616 fight¦35615 together¦35615 and¦35617 strike¦35617 a¦35618 pregnant¦35619 woman and¦35620 \add ≈cause her¦35621 to deliver the baby¦35621\add*, if there's no serious injury¦35624, then he must¦35623 certainly¦35625 be¦35626 fined¦35626 whatever the¦35630 husband¦35630 of¦35630 the¦35631 woman¦35631 \add ≈demands\add* and¦35632 the¦35633 judges allow.
\v 23 But¦35635 if¦35635 there's serious injury¦35637, then¦35639 you¦35639 must¦35639 give¦35639 a¦35640 life¦35640 for a¦35642 life¦35642,
\v 24 an¦35644 eye¦35644 for an¦35646 eye¦35646, a¦35647 tooth¦35647 for a¦35649 tooth¦35649, a¦35650 hand¦35650 for a¦35652 hand¦35652, a¦35653 foot¦35653 for a¦35655 foot¦35655,\x + \xo 21:24: \xt Lev 24:19-20; Deu 19:21; Mat 5:38.\x*
\v 25 a burn¦35657 for a burn¦35659, a wound for a wound, and a bruise for a bruise.
\p
\v 26 If a master hits¦35670 their male¦35675 or¦35676 female¦35681 slave's¦35681 eye and¦35682 damages it¦35682, they must¦35684 let \add @the¦35684 slave¦35684\add* go free¦35684 in¦35685 compensation for the eye¦35686.
\v 27 \add Or\add* if¦35689 the¦35691 master knocks¦35697 out¦35697 a tooth¦35691 of¦35691 a male¦35692 or¦35693 female¦35696 slave¦35696, they must¦35699 let \add @the¦35699 slave¦35699\add* go free¦35699 in¦35700 compensation for the tooth¦35701.
\s1 Rules about property
\rem /s1 Laws concerning Property; The Responsibility of Owners
\p
\v 28 If a bull¦35707 gores¦35706 a¦35710 man¦35710 or¦35711 a¦35714 woman¦35714 and¦35715 they die¦35715, the bull must certainly¦35716 be¦35717 \add ≈killed by throwing rocks at it\add*. The¦35724 owner¦35724 of¦35724 the¦35725 animal is \add to be considered\add* innocent, \add but\add* its¦35723 meat¦35723 mustn't be¦35726 eaten¦35720.
\v 29 But¦35728 if¦35728 the bull¦35729 had a habit of goring¦35730 in the past and¦35734 its owner was¦35734 warned¦35734 but¦35736 didn't¦35736 keep it¦35737 \add fenced\add* in, and¦35738 it¦35738 kills¦35738 a¦35739 man¦35739 or¦35740 a¦35741 woman¦35741, then the¦35742 bull¦35742 must¦35743 be¦35743 \add ≈killed by throwing rocks¦35743 at it\add* and¦35744 its¦35746 owner must¦35747 also¦35744 be¦35747 put¦35747 to¦35747 death¦35747.
\v 30 If¦35749 a¦35751 ransom¦35751 payment is¦35752 demanded instead, \add @the owner\add* may \add ≈buy back\add* their life¦35756 by paying the full amount demanded.
\v 31 Those rules also apply if the bull gores a¦35765 \add person's\add* son¦35765 or¦35767 daughter¦35769.
\v 32 If¦35776 the¦35780 bull¦35780 gores¦35779 a¦35778 male¦35778 or¦35781 female¦35782 slave¦35782, \add @its¦35787 owner¦35787\add* must¦35787 give¦35787 thirty¦35785 silver¦35783 shekels¦35786 to¦35788 the \add slave owner\add* and¦35789 the¦35789 bull¦35789 must¦35790 be¦35790 \add ≈killed by throwing rocks at it\add*.
\p
\v 33 If¦35799 someone¦35796 uncovers a¦35797 \add pit for storage\add*, or¦35798 digs¦35801 a¦35803 \add new one\add* and¦35804 leaves it¦35805 uncovered, and¦35806 a cow¦35809 or¦35810 a¦35811 donkey¦35811 falls¦35806 into¦35808 it,
\v 34 the¦35813 owner¦35813 of¦35813 the¦35814 pit¦35814 must¦35815 pay compensation¦35815. \add @The¦35817 pit¦35817 owner¦35817\add* must¦35817 pay in cash¦35816, \add but\add* the¦35819 dead¦35819 animal will¦35820 then¦35819 belong¦35820 to them.
\v 35 If one person's bull¦35828 attacks¦35827 \add someone else's\add* bull¦35833 and¦35835 it¦35835 dies¦35835, then¦35836 they¦35836 should sell¦35836 the¦35839 live¦35840 animal and¦35841 divide¦35841 the money¦35844 as well as dividing up the¦35848 dead¦35848 animal.
\v 36 \add But\add* if¦35853 it¦35852 was¦35852 known¦35852 that¦35853 the bull¦35854 had a habit of goring¦35855 in the past and¦35859 its owner¦35861 hadn't kept it¦35860 \add fenced\add* in, then \add @that¦35861 owner¦35861\add* must certainly¦35862 give a live bull¦35864 for the¦35866 dead one, \add but\add* the¦35867 dead¦35867 animal will¦35868 become theirs.
\c 22
\s1 Rules about loss of property or farm animals
\rem /s1 Laws of Restitution; Protection of Property; Laws about Repayment
\p
\v 1 If¦35874 someone¦35877 steals¦35875 a cow¦35878 or¦35879 a¦35881 sheep¦35881 and¦35882 slaughters¦35882 it¦35882 or¦35883 sells¦35884 it¦35884, he must compensate five¦35885 cattle¦35886 for the¦35889 cow¦35889 or four¦35890 sheep¦35892 for the¦35894 sheep¦35894.
\p
\v 2 (If¦35897 a thief¦35901 is¦35900 discovered¦35900 \add at night\add* while breaking in, and¦35902 is¦35902 struck¦35902 and¦35903 dies¦35903, \add ≈no one should be¦35904 charged for murder\add*.
\v 3 But if¦35909 it's daylight already, then that person is guilty of murder.)
\p \add @A thief\add* who's caught must¦35917 make¦35917 restitution¦35917, but if¦35918 they're unable to¦35921 pay, then¦35922 \add @the¦35922 thief¦35922\add* must¦35922 be¦35922 sold¦35922 \add to cover it\add*.
\v 4 If¦35926 a thief is found in¦35930 the¦35931 possession of¦35930 a stolen¦35931 animal, be it a cow¦35932 or a¦35935 donkey¦35935 or a¦35938 sheep¦35938, then they must¦35941 pay back another matching animal as well.\f + \fr 22:4 \ft Or possibly, must pay back \+bd two\+bd* additional animals.\f*
\p
\v 5 If¦35945 a person releases¦35953 their livestock¦35956 to¦35946 graze¦35946 \add their own\add* field¦35949 or¦35950 vineyard¦35952, and¦35958 it¦35958 \add ends up\add* grazing¦35958 in¦35959 the¦35959 someone¦35948 else's field¦35959, \add @the owner of the animal\add* must compensate from the¦35961 best¦35961 of¦35961 their field¦35962 and¦35963 from the¦35963 best¦35963 of¦35963 their vineyard¦35964.
\p
\v 6 If¦35969 a¦35972 fire¦35972 breaks out¦35971 and¦35973 spreads in \add ≈the¦35978 bush\add*, and grain¦35978 that's already stacked¦35976 or¦35979 is¦35975 still in the¦35980 field¦35980 gets burnt up, the¦35983 person who¦35983 started¦35983 the¦35986 fire¦35986 must certainly¦35981 pay compensation.
\p
\v 7 If¦35990 anyone¦35993 gives¦35992 their neighbour¦35996 money¦35997 or¦35998 goods for safekeeping, and¦36002 if it¦36002 is¦36002 stolen¦36002 from¦36003 the¦36003 neighbour's house¦36003, if¦36005 the¦36008 thief¦36008 is¦36007 found¦36007, they must¦36009 pay double¦36010 in compensation.
\v 8 If¦36013 the¦36017 thief¦36017 can't¦36015 be¦36016 found¦36016, then¦36018 the¦36019 owner¦36019 of¦36019 the¦36021 house¦36021 must¦36018 be¦36018 brought¦36018 to¦36022 \add ?≈the¦36024 judges for God's decision\add* as to whether or not¦36027 they have¦36028 \add ≈improperly handled\add* their neighbour's¦36031 property¦36030.
\p
\v 9 Concerning every¦36036 other squabble about any cattle or a¦36046 donkey¦36046 or sheep¦36049, or about clothing¦36052 or any lost¦36057 property that's¦36058 under dispute, both¦36069 parties must go to¦36064 \add ?≈the judges¦36072 for God's decision\add*—anyone¦36059 who¦36070 God declares to be guilty¦36071 must¦36073 pay his¦36075 neighbour¦36075 double¦36074 as compensation.
\p
