\v 20 Then¦43060 all¦43061 the Israelis¦43066 who'd been assembled in front¦43067 of Mosheh¦43068 went¦43060 away,
\v 21 and¦43070 everyone¦43071 who¦43074 \add ≈felt to do so¦43078\add* came¦43070 \add back\add* bringing¦43083 their \add #contributions¦43086\add* to Yahweh¦43087 for¦43088 the¦43088 work¦43088 on the¦43089 sacred tent¦43089 and¦43091 all¦43091 its¦43093 components, and¦43094 for¦43094 the¦43094 \add ≈sacred\add* clothes¦43094.
\v 22 This included both men¦43098 and women¦43101 who¦43118 were \add ≈wanting to contribute\add*, and they¦43106 brought¦43106 necklaces and¦43108 earrings¦43108 and ornaments¦43110—all¦43111 made of¦43113 gold¦43114. They held them up high as a¦43120 raised offering of¦43120 gold¦43121 to¦43122 Yahweh¦43122.
\v 23 \add ≈Many¦43124 people¦43124\add* who¦43127 had blue or purple¦43132 or scarlet yarn, or finely-spun linen¦43135 or goats¦43136' hair¦43136, reddened ram¦43138 skins¦43140 or sea-cow hides¦43137 brought¦43142 \add <them\add*.
\v 24 \add ≈Others who¦43146 could,\add* brought a¦43147 contribution¦43147 of¦43147 silver¦43148 or bronze¦43149 to Yahweh¦43153, and¦43154 those¦43146 with¦43157 acacia¦43159 wood¦43158 brought \add <it\add* for all¦43160 the¦43162 \add ≈construction of¦43162 the¦43163 tent components\add*.
\v 25 All¦43166 the \add #women¦43168\add* who were \add ≈skilled¦43169 at making cloth\add* brought¦43174 blue, purple¦43181, or scarlet yarn and¦43186 fine¦43188, hand-spun linen¦43188 thread,
\v 26 and¦43190 all¦43190 those women¦43192 who¦43193 \add ≈felt to do so\add* spun¦43198 goats¦43201' hair¦43201.
\v 27 The¦43203 leaders¦43203 brought¦43204 onyx¦43207 stones and other gemstones to be mounted¦43210 on the sacred apron¦43211 and¦43212 for¦43212 the sacred pouch,
\v 28 along with the¦43216 \add #spices¦43216\add* and¦43217 \add olive\add* oil¦43219 to be used for the¦43221 maps, the¦43222 anointing¦43222 oil, and¦43223 the¦43223 fragrant¦43224 incense¦43223.
\v 29 ⇔\add Thus\add* the Israelis¦43248 brought a¦43249 voluntary offering¦43249 to¦43250 Yahweh¦43250—every¦43226 man¦43228 and¦43229 woman¦43229 who¦43230 \add ≈felt to¦43234 do so\add* brought¦43234 something for all¦43235 the¦43237 work¦43237 which¦43238 Yahweh¦43240 had¦43239 instructed¦43239 them through¦43242 Mosheh¦43244 to do.
\s1 The skills of Bezalel and Oholiab
\rem /s1 Bezalel and Oholiab; Workers to Make the Tent of the Lord's Presence
\r (Exo. 31:1-11)
\p
\v 30 Then¦43253 Mosheh¦43254 said¦43253 to¦43255 the¦43257 Israelis¦43258, “Listen, Yahweh¦43261 has¦43260 called¦43260 Bezalel¦43263 by name¦43262 (the son of¦43267 Hur's¦43269 son Uri¦43266, from the¦43270 tribe¦43270 of Judah¦43271)
\v 31 and¦43273 he¦43273 has¦43273 filled¦43273 him¦43274 with \add *his\add* spirit¦43275, with¦43277 skill¦43277 and understanding¦43278, and¦43279 with¦43279 knowledge¦43279 and¦43280 with all¦43280 kinds of craftsmanship¦43282.
\v 32 \add He can\add* design¦43284 designs¦43285, work¦43286 in¦43287 gold¦43287, silver¦43288, and¦43289 bronze¦43289,
\v 33 in¦43291 cutting gemstones¦43292 for¦43296 setting, and¦43294 in¦43294 wood-carving—to¦43293 do¦43296 all¦43297 kinds of¦43299 design¦43300 and crafting.
\v 34 \add @Yahweh\add* has also¦43302 \add ≈given him the desire\add* to¦43302 teach¦43302, along with Oholiab¦43306 (Ahisamak's son¦43307 from the¦43310 tribe¦43310 of Dan¦43312).
\v 35 He's¦43314 given them¦43315 \add both\add* skills to do¦43319 all¦43320 the¦43322 work¦43322 of¦43322 craftsmen and¦43325 of skillful¦43325 workmen and of embroiderers in blue, purple¦43328, and scarlet yarn, and¦43331 in¦43331 the fine linen¦43331 and¦43332 of weavers¦43332—\add both\add* as designers¦43337 as well as the various, \add actual\add* crafts¦43336.
\c 36
\nb
\v 1 Bezalel¦43341 and¦43342 Oholiab¦43342 will do¦43356 the¦43361 work¦43361 of¦43361 constructing all the¦43362 components of¦43362 the¦43363 sacred¦43363 tent along with any¦43364 others who are \add ≈like-minded\add* and who have been given the necessary \add #skills¦43352\add* and¦43353 understanding¦43353 by Yahweh¦43368.”
\s1 More than enough materials donated
\rem /s1 The People Bring Many Gifts
\p
\v 2 \add ≈So¦43370\add* Mosheh¦43371 summoned¦43370 Bezalel¦43374 and¦43376 Oholiab¦43378, as well as anyone else \add ≈who felt to help\add* and had¦43387 been given the¦43398 \add appropriate\add* \add #skills¦43389\add* by Yahweh¦43388,
\v 3 and¦43402 they¦43402 took¦43402 \add away\add* all¦43406 the¦43408 \add #contributions¦43408\add* that¦43409 the¦43411 Israelis¦43412 had brought to Mosheh¦43404 to be used for¦43413 the construction of the sacred¦43415 tent. \add ≈However\add* \add @the people\add* \add still\add* continued to¦43420 bring voluntary \add #offerings¦43422\add* every morning.
\v 4 \add ≈So¦43426\add* all¦43427 those skilled¦43429 \add people\add* came¦43426 \add over\add* to \add start on\add* the¦43434 work¦43434 of¦43434 \add making\add* the¦43435 sacred tent, Holy¦43435 Place¦43435 came, \add ≈leaving behind\add* the work¦43439 that¦43440 they¦43442 had been¦43443 doing¦43443,
\v 5 and¦43445 they¦43445 \add ≈informed\add* Mosheh¦43448, “The¦43451 people¦43451 are¦43450 bringing¦43452 way more than¦43453 enough¦43453 to use for¦43455 the work¦43455 that¦43456 Yahweh¦43459 has¦43458 instructed¦43458 us to¦43460 do¦43460.”
\p
\v 6 \add ≈So¦43463\add* with Mosheh's¦43464 \add ≈agreement\add*, they¦43465 spread a¦43466 message around the camp¦43467 saying¦43468, “\add ≈Please don't¦43471 bring\add* any more \add #contributions¦43477\add* for¦43477 the sacred¦43478 tent.” \add ≈So¦43479\add* the¦43480 people¦43480—\add #men¦43469\add* and¦43470 \add #women¦43470\add*—stopped¦43479 bringing¦43481 \add <donations\add*
\v 7 as \add they had already collected\add* more¦43491 than¦43486 enough¦43491 materials for all¦43486 the¦43488 work¦43488.
\s1 The construction of the tent
\rem /s1 Making the Tent of the Lord's Presence; Building the Tabernacle; Construction of the Tabernacle; The Tabernacle
\r (Exo. 26:1-37)
\p
\v 8 Then¦43494 all¦43495 the \add ≈most-skilled men\add* out of the¦43501 workmen made¦43501 the¦43504 ten¦43505 \add strips of\add* curtaining¦43506 for the residence from twisted¦43508 finely-spun linen¦43507 and blue, purple¦43510, scarlet yarn \add used to embroider\add* winged-creatures on them¦43517.
\v 9 Each \add ≈strip\add* was¦43522 14.5m long¦43519 and two metres wide¦43525—they were¦43526 all the same size¦43530.
\v 10 Five¦43539 \add ≈strips\add* were sewn together as one set, and¦43545 the¦43540 other five¦43545 as another set.
\v 11 \add #They\add*\f + \fr 36:11 \ft It's singular ‘he’ in the original, which is confusing after v8 mentioned a group of craftsmen. The \+em OET-RV\+em* has chosen to follow v8 (and change the pronoun to ‘they’ in this section), but other translations are divided in how they handle this dilemma.\f* made¦43553 loops¦43554 of¦43554 blue on¦43556 the¦43557 edge¦43557 of¦43557 the¦43558 first curtain¦43558 at the¦43564 edge¦43564 of¦43564 the set, and similarly for the second set—
\v 12 fifty \add matching\add* loops on the edge of¦43578 each set¦43581 so¦43575 they were¦43583 aligned with each other.
\v 13 \add #They\add* \add also\add* made¦43590 fifty¦43591 gold¦43593 clips and¦43594 used them to join¦43594 the¦43597 \add two\add* curtain¦43597 \add sets\add* to each other to make \add the wall of\add* the¦43604 \add ≈sacred¦43604 tent¦43604\add*.
\p
\v 14 Then¦43608 \add #they\add* made¦43608 eleven goats¦43610' hair¦43610 curtain sections for the tarpaulin over¦43612 the¦43614 \add ≈sacred¦43614 tent¦43614\add*.
\v 15 All eleven \add ≈sections\add* were¦43625 the¦43622 same size¦43632 at fifteen metres long¦43622 and¦43627 two metres¦43628 wide¦43629,
\v 16 and¦43638 they joined¦43638 five¦43641 \add ≈sections\add* to each other \add for one set\add* and¦43644 six¦43646 for the¦43647 other.
\v 17 Then \add #they\add* \add ≈attached\add* fifty \add blue\add* loops onto one edge of each of the two sets¦43665
\v 18 and¦43668 made¦43668 fifty¦43671 bronze¦43670 clips for connecting¦43672 them into¦43676 one¦43677 \add continuous\add* cover.
\v 19 \add #They\add* then¦43679 made¦43679 a¦43680 tarpaulin from red¦43684 rams¦43683 skins¦43686 to go over the tent¦43681, and¦43685 the top¦43685 \add waterproof\add* tarpaulin of sea-cow hides¦43682.
\p
\v 20 Then¦43691 \add #they\add* made¦43691 \add forty-eight\add* upright¦43698 frames¦43694 from acacia¦43697 wood¦43696 for¦43695 the \add ≈sacred¦43695 tent¦43695\add*.
\v 21 Each frame was¦43700 five metres¦43701 long¦43702 and 0.75m wide¦43707
\v 22 with two¦43711 protrusions on each frame¦43713 for connecting¦43715 them together, so¦43720 all¦43722 the¦43723 frames¦43723 were made in this way.
\v 23 \add #They\add* made¦43726 twenty¦43731 frames¦43732 for¦43733 the¦43734 south¦43734 side¦43733
\v 24 with their forty¦43737 silver¦43740 bases¦43746—two bases¦43754 under¦43755 each frame¦43757 that two¦43759 \add more\add* protrusions fitted \add down\add* into,
\v 25 then¦43762 two \add more\add* for the¦43766 north¦43766 side
\v 26 with their forty¦43771 silver¦43773 bases¦43775—two¦43779 bases¦43780 under¦43781 each frame¦43782.
\v 27 Six¦43789 frames¦43790 were made¦43788 for¦43785 the west¦43787 side
\v 28 as well as two¦43792 frames¦43793 for¦43795 the¦43795 back corners¦43795 of¦43795 the¦43796 \add ≈sacred¦43796 tent¦43796\add*.
\v 29 ⇔At the¦43815 two¦43815 corners¦43816, \add @the frames\add* were connected together¦43802 at the bottom, and \add similarly\add* at the top before being¦43803 attached to¦43805 a single¦43811 ring¦43810
\v 30 \add ≈so¦43818\add* that made eight¦43819 frames¦43820 with their¦43821 sixteen silver¦43822 bases—two bases under¦43830 each frame¦43831.
\p
\v 31 Then¦43834 \add #they\add* made¦43834 crossbars¦43835 from acacia¦43837 wood¦43836—five¦43838 for the¦43839 frames¦43839 on the¦43840 \add north\add* side¦43840 of¦43840 the¦43842 \add ≈sacred¦43842 tent¦43842\add*,
\v 32 five¦43845 for the \add ≈south\add* side¦43848, and¦43852 five¦43852 crossbars¦43853 for the¦43854 frames¦43854 on the¦43855 west¦43857 \add side\add* along the back.
\v 33 The¦43863 middle¦43863 crossbar was made¦43859 to¦43864 pass¦43864 through¦43864 from \add one\add* end to \add ≈the other\add* in¦43865 the¦43865 centre¦43865 of¦43866 the¦43866 frames¦43866,
\v 34 and¦43874 the¦43876 frames¦43876 were overlaid¦43877 with gold¦43878. \add #They\add* then¦43879 made¦43882 gold¦43883 rings¦43881 to be the holders¦43884 for¦43885 the crossbars¦43885, and¦43886 \add *they\add* \add too\add* were overlaid¦43886 with gold¦43890.
\p
\v 35 \add #They\add* made¦43892 a curtain¦43895 from twisted¦43901 finely-spun linen¦43900, and a¦43903 skillful¦43903 workman embroidered winged creatures on them with blue, purple¦43897, scarlet yarn.
\v 36 \add #They\add* made¦43908 four¦43910 pillars¦43911 of¦43911 acacia¦43912 wood overlaid¦43913 with gold¦43914, as well as their¦43915 gold¦43916 hooks¦43915 and¦43917 four¦43919 bases¦43920 cast¦43917 from silver¦43922.
\v 37 \add #They\add* made¦43924 a¦43925 curtain for¦43926 the¦43927 tent¦43927 entrance¦43926—an embroiderer made it from twisted¦43933 finely-spun linen¦43932 and¦43929 blue, purple¦43929, and¦43930 scarlet yarn,
\v 38 along with its¦43939 five¦43940 pillars and¦43941 their¦43943 hooks¦43943. The tops¦43945 of¦43945 the pillars were overlaid¦43944 with gold¦43947, and¦43948 their¦43948 five¦43949 bases¦43948 \add were made of\add* bronze¦43950.
\c 37
\s1 Making the sacred box
\rem /s1 Making the Covenant Box; Making the Ark of the Covenant; The Ark; Building the Ark
\r (Exo. 25:10-22)
\p
\v 1 Then¦43953 Bezalel¦43954 made¦43953 the¦43957 \add sacred\add* chest¦43957 from acacia¦43959 wood¦43958—it was¦43960 1.2m long¦43962, 0.75m wide¦43965, and 0.75m high¦43968.
\v 2 He¦43970 overlaid¦43970 \add both\add* the inside¦43973 and¦43974 the outside¦43974 with pure¦43972 gold, and¦43975 made¦43975 a¦43977 gold moulding¦43977 all¦43979 around¦43979 it,
\v 3 and¦43981 he¦43981 cast¦43981 four¦43983 rings¦43984 of¦43984 gold¦43985 \add and attached them\add* to its four¦43987 feet¦43988—two¦43989 rings¦43990 on¦43991 each one¦43994 side¦43999.
\v 4 He¦44002 made¦44002 \add two\add* acacia¦44005 wood¦44004 poles¦44003 and¦44006 overlaid¦44006 them¦44007 with gold¦44008,
\v 5 and¦44010 he¦44010 put the¦44013 poles¦44013 into¦44014 the rings¦44014 on¦44015 the¦44016 sides¦44016 of¦44016 the¦44017 box¦44017 for carrying¦44018 it.
\p
\v 6 Then¦44023 he¦44023 made¦44023 the atonement¦44024 platform from pure¦44026 gold¦44025 \add to fit\add* as the lid for the box, \add so\add* it was¦44027 \add also\add* 1.2m long¦44029 and 0.75m wide¦44032,
\v 7 ⇔then¦44034 from the¦44042 ends¦44042 of¦44042 the lid, he hammered¦44038 the gold¦44037 to form two¦44041 winged creatures.
\v 8 There was¦44048 a¦44050 winged creature sitting on each end¦44053 of the¦44057 atonement¦44057 platform made¦44058 from¦44062 its two¦44062 ends¦44063,
\v 9 and¦44066 they¦44066 were¦44066 spreading¦44068 their wings¦44069 upwards¦44070 to cover¦44071 over¦44073 the¦44075 platform. They faced¦44076 each¦44077 other, \add ≈looking down towards\add* the¦44083 atonement¦44083 platform.
\s1 Making the bread table
\rem /s1 Making the Table for the Bread of the Presence; Making the Table for the Bread Offered to God; The Table; Building the Table
\r (Exo. 25:23-30)
\p
\v 10 He¦44089 made¦44089 \add the bread\add* table¦44092 from acacia¦44094 wood¦44093—one metre long¦44096, half¦44100 a¦44097 metre wide¦44098, and three-quarters of a¦44099 metre high¦44101.
\v 11 It was overlaid¦44103 with pure¦44106 gold and¦44107 had a¦44109 gold moulding¦44109 all¦44111 around¦44111 it.
\v 12 He¦44113 made¦44113 an 8cm wide¦44116 rim¦44115 all¦44117 around¦44117 it, and a¦44119 gold¦44121 moulding¦44119 around the rim.
\v 13 He¦44125 moulded four¦44127 gold rings¦44128 from gold¦44129 and¦44130 attached them to the¦44135 four¦44135 corners¦44136 of the table. Each ring was close to each table leg
\v 14 near¦44141 the¦44142 rim¦44142 to hold the¦44144 poles¦44146 for carrying¦44147 the¦44150 table¦44150.
\v 15 He¦44152 made¦44152 \add two\add* acacia¦44157 wood¦44156 poles¦44155 and¦44158 overlaid¦44158 them¦44159 for gold¦44160—they were for carrying¦44161 the¦44164 table¦44164.
\v 16 He¦44166 \add also\add* made¦44166 the¦44169 utensils¦44169 that¦44171 would be¦44172 on¦44172 the¦44174 table¦44174—its¦44177 plates¦44177 and¦44178 pans¦44180, and¦44181 its¦44182 bowls¦44182 and¦44183 pitchers¦44185 to be¦44187 used for pouring¦44187 out¦44187 \add <the wine offerings\add*—all made from pure¦44190 gold¦44189.
\s1 Making the lampstand
\rem /s1 Building the Lampstand; The Lampstand; Making the Lampstand
\r (Exo. 25:31-40)
\p
\v 17 Then¦44193 he¦44193 made¦44193 the¦44196 lampstand¦44196 from pure¦44198 gold¦44197. The¦44203 base¦44204 and¦44205 shaft¦44205 of¦44205 the lampstand were hammered¦44199 \add ≈into shape\add*, along with its¦44205 cups¦44206, and its¦44206 buds¦44207 and¦44208 blossoms¦44208.
\v 18 There were six¦44212 branches¦44213 going¦44214 out¦44214 from¦44215 the shaft of the lampstand—three on each side¦44225.
\v 19 Each branch¦44231 had three¦44235 gold cups¦44236 on¦44238 it that¦44245 looked like¦44237 almond¦44237 flowers¦44237 with buds and¦44241 petals. It was the same for¦44243 each of¦44243 the¦44248 lampstand's¦44248 six¦44243 branches¦44244.
\v 20 On¦44250 the lampstand¦44250 \add shaft\add* there were four¦44251 cups¦44252 that also looked like¦44253 almond¦44253 blossoms¦44253, each one with flower buds¦44254 and¦44255 petals.
\v 21 He made one flower bud beneath¦44258 every pair of¦44259 branches \add (that were going out from opposite sides)\add*. The bud was¦44263 attached to each pair of¦44264 branches¦44265 as if they were¦44268 growing out¦44275 of¦44270 it¦44272—all six¦44273 lampstand branches¦44274 were attached like that¦44275.
\v 22 The buds¦44278 and¦44279 the branches¦44279 coming out of¦44279 them were¦44281 all¦44282 hammered¦44283 out of one¦44284 piece of pure¦44286 gold¦44285.
\v 23 Then¦44288 he¦44288 made¦44288 the seven¦44292 lamps¦44291 out of¦44291 pure¦44296 gold¦44295, along with the tongs¦44293 and¦44294 trays¦44294.
\v 24 He¦44301 made¦44301 \add @the¦44302 lampstand¦44302\add* and tongs and trays using 33kg of pure¦44300 gold¦44299.
\s1 Making the incense altar
\rem /s1 Building the Incense Altar; Making the Altar of Incense; Making the Altar for Burning Incense
\r (Exo. 30:1-5)
\p
\v 25 Then¦44309 he¦44309 made¦44309 the¦44312 altar¦44312 \add for burning\add* incense¦44313 out of¦44314 acacia¦44315 wood¦44314. It was¦44316 square¦44320—half a¦44316 metre on each side—and¦44318 one metre high¦44322. Its¦44319 projections \add on the top four corners\add* were¦44321 part of¦44322 it¦44323.
\v 26 He¦44327 overlaid¦44327 it¦44328 with pure¦44330 gold—all four sides¦44336 including¦44338 the projections—and¦44341 put a¦44343 gold moulding¦44343 all¦44345 around¦44345 it.
\v 27 Then¦44347 he¦44350 made¦44350 two gold¦44349 rings¦44348 \add and attached them\add* below the moulding¦44355—two¦44357 on¦44359 each side¦44361 for holding the poles¦44363 for carrying¦44364 \add @the¦44365 altar¦44365\add*.
\v 28 The¦44371 \add two\add* poles¦44371 were made¦44368 from acacia¦44373 wood¦44372 and¦44374 then overlaid¦44374 with gold¦44376.
\rem /s1 Making the Anointing Oil and the Incense
\rem /r (Exodus 30.22-38)
\v 29 He¦44378 got a \add professional\add* ointment maker to make¦44378 the sacred¦44383 anointing¦44382 oil¦44381 and¦44384 the pure¦44388, fragrant¦44387 incense¦44386.\x + \xo 37:29: \xt Exo 30:22-38.\x*
\c 38
\s1 Making the altar for burning sacrifices
\rem /s1 The Altar of Burnt Offering; Building the Altar of Burnt Offering; Making the Altar for Burning Offerings; Making the Altar of Burnt Offering
\r (Exo. 27:1-8)
\p
\v 1 Then¦44393 \add @Bezalel¦44393\add* made¦44393 the¦44396 altar¦44396 for burning¦44397 \add #offerings¦44397\add* from acacia¦44399 wood¦44398. It was square¦44407—2.5m on each side—and¦44408 1.5m tall.
\v 2 \add ≈From the same piece of wood\add*, he¦44412 made¦44412 horn-like projections for each of¦44413 the¦44415 four¦44415 corners¦44416, then¦44420 he¦44420 overlaid¦44420 it¦44421 all with bronze¦44422.
\v 3 He¦44424 made¦44424 all¦44427 the¦44429 utensils¦44429 for the¦44430 altar¦44430: the¦44433 pots¦44433 and¦44434 shovels¦44436, basins¦44439 and¦44437 meat-forks, and¦44443 the¦44445 firepans¦44445—all¦44446 made¦44449 from bronze¦44450.
\v 4 He¦44452 made¦44452 a¦44454 grating¦44454 for the altar—a bronze¦44457 lattice \add that fitted\add* \add ≈down in the middle¦44463 of¦44463 the altar\add*,
\v 5 and¦44465 he¦44465 cast¦44465 the¦44470 grating¦44470 with rings¦44467 on¦44468 the¦44468 four¦44468 corners as holders¦44472 for¦44473 the \add carrying\add* poles¦44473.
\v 6 He¦44475 made¦44475 the¦44478 acacia¦44480 wood¦44479 poles¦44478 and¦44481 overlaid¦44481 them¦44482 with bronze¦44483
\v 7 and¦44485 he¦44485 put the¦44488 poles¦44488 into¦44489 the rings¦44489 on¦44490 the¦44491 sides¦44491 of¦44491 the¦44492 altar¦44492 so they could carry¦44493 it¦44494 with them¦44495. He¦44498 made¦44498 \add @the¦44499 altar¦44499\add* hollow¦44496 using boards¦44497.
